common_name\": \"methacrolein\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_014\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H8O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-methylpropanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"isobutyraldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C4H8O/c1-4(2)3-5/h3-4H,1-2H3\",\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Hydrothermal\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_015\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H9NO\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-hydroxy-3-methylbutanenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"isobutyraldehyde cyanohydrin\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C5H9NO/c1-4(2)5(7)3-6/h4-5,7H,1-2H3\",\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_016\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H9N\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-methylpropan-1-imine\",\n      \"common_name\": \"isobutyraldimine\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_017\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H10N2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-amino-3-methylbutanenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"valine aminonitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C5H10N2/c1-4(2)5(7)3-6/h4-5H,7H2,1-2H3\",\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_018\",\n      \"formula\": \"C6H10N2O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"5-(propan-2-yl)imidazolidine-2,4-dione\",\n      \"common_name\": \"5-isopropylhydantoin\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_019\",\n      \"formula\": \"S8\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"cyclooctasulfur\",\n      \"common_name\": \"elemental sulfur\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_020\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H11NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-amino-3-methylbutanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"valine, racemic\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C5H11NO2/c1-3(2)4(6)5(7)8/h3-4H,6H2,1-2H3,(H,7,8)\",\n      \"role\": \"target\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"Acetone cyanohydrin formation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_007\",\n        \"mol_002\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_011\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic aqueous pool; mildly alkaline conditions provide some cyanide while retaining HCN. Concentration and protection from rapid volatilization are required; UV is not required for this addition.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Reversible cyanide addition to the acetone carbonyl followed by protonation produces the tertiary cyanohydrin.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is chemically conventional cyanohydrin chemistry and is the acetone-entry reaction attributed to Patel et al. (2015) in the supplied review. No mineral is needed. Equilibrium, HCN loss, and competing cyanide chemistry limit accumulation; the review does not establish a general environmental yield.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Sulfide addition to acetone cyanohydrin nitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_011\",\n        \"mol_004\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_012\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic sulfide-containing solution, approximately pH 8-9; H2S and HS- are represented by their neutral parent species.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrosulfide attacks the nitrile carbon; proton transfer and tautomerization generate the thioamide.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The supplied review attributes this nitrile-to-thioamide transformation to Patel et al. (2015). H2S is a consumed reagent, not an unexplained catalytic agent. Cyanohydrin dissociation and competing sulfide reactions remain relevant; substrate-specific rates and yields are not independently verified here.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Hypothetical cyanosulfidic deoxygenative homologation and cyanide trapping\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_012\",\n        \"mol_002\",\n        \"mol_004\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_015\",\n        \"mol_003\",\n        \"mol_019\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Cu(I)-cyanide photoredox complexes, proposed for this specific transformation\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Cu(I)-cyanide photoredox complexes\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic UV-irradiated cyanide/sulfide solution; staged chemistry rather than an assumed one-pot process. Copper speciation, sulfide precipitation, and sulfur-product speciation require experimental determination.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Unresolved net sequence requiring removal of the tertiary hydroxyl functionality, conversion of the original thioamide carbon into an aldehyde-equivalent carbon, loss of the original nitrogen as ammonia, and capture by a new cyanide. Elemental sulfur is an idealized terminal oxidation sink; transient polysulfides are not resolved.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"SPECULATIVE: the supplied report attributes a related transformation to Patel et al. (2015), but its displayed equation loses nitrogen and cannot establish the proposed mechanism. This replacement is atom-balanced with inferred coefficients and explicitly includes ammonia, but balancing does not demonstrate feasibility. The first cyanide-derived carbon becomes the eventual valine alpha carbon; the second becomes its carboxyl carbon. Cu(I) photoredox catalysis is chemically distinct from claiming dissolved elemental copper. Aldehyde formation, productive reduction, sulfur products, and selectivity must be demonstrated. This is the decisive unresolved bottleneck for P1 and P2.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Crossed aldol addition and dehydration of propanal with formaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_008\",\n        \"mol_009\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_013\",\n        \"mol_001\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"hydroxide as a general-base catalyst\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"315-350 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"hydroxide\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic alkaline pool, approximately pH 9-11; concentration cycles may favor condensation. Mixed-product formation is expected rather than selective methacrolein production.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Propanal enolate adds to formaldehyde, producing a hydroxymethylated aldehyde that dehydrates to methacrolein. This node represents the net aldol/dehydration sequence.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"SPECULATIVE as a prebiotic feedstock-supply reaction; it is not supported by the supplied Patel et al. (2015) review. Standard crossed-aldol chemistry provides a carbon-conserving way to construct the required branched C4 skeleton. Propanal self-condensation, formaldehyde disproportionation, repeated hydroxymethylation, and polymerization compete. Selectivity under the stated geochemical conditions is unestablished.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Candidate selective hydrogenation of methacrolein to isobutyraldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_013\",\n        \"mol_005\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_014\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"native Ni-Fe alloy surface, such as awaruite, as a candidate hydrogenation catalyst\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-420 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"10-100 bar, with sufficient H2 activity\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"native Ni-Fe alloy\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic, locally sulfide-poor rock-water interface with accessible metallic sites; effluent must cool before downstream cyanide chemistry.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Surface activation of hydrogen followed by addition across the carbon-carbon double bond would preserve the aldehyde group and produce isobutyraldehyde.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"SPECULATIVE: no substrate-specific prebiotic demonstration is supplied. Ni-containing metallic surfaces are chosen for hydrogen activation and alkene hydrogenation, not merely because they are minerals. The crucial unresolved issue is selectivity: carbonyl reduction, full conversion to alcohols, hydrogenolysis, and surface poisoning may prevent aldehyde accumulation. This bottleneck conditions all aldol-derived pathways.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Candidate isobutanol dehydrogenation to isobutyraldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_010\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_014\",\n        \"mol_005\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"native copper surface as a candidate heterogeneous dehydrogenation catalyst\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"450-520 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"50-150 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"heterogeneous Cu(0)\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic, sulfide-poor, H2-poor flowing hydrothermal zone; product removal and rapid effluent cooling are required. This is not assigned to an H2-rich serpentinization reactor.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Catalytic removal of hydrogen from the alcohol O-H bond and adjacent C-H bond forms the aldehyde and molecular hydrogen without molecular oxygen.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"SPECULATIVE geochemical application of an established alcohol-dehydrogenation reaction family; the supplied research does not document this reaction. Copper here is a solid catalyst, not dissolved Cu(0) or an unspecified stoichiometric reductant. H2-rich conditions oppose dehydrogenation, while sulfide can deactivate copper. Adequate isobutanol supply, reaction rate in water, and aldehyde survival remain unresolved.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Isobutyraldehyde cyanohydrin formation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_014\",\n        \"mol_002\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_015\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic concentrated solution, approximately pH 7-9; retention of volatile aldehyde and HCN is necessary. Shaded pore water can limit photochemical destruction between irradiation episodes.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Reversible cyanide addition to isobutyraldehyde followed by protonation gives the C5 alpha-hydroxynitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is a conventional cyanohydrin entry to Strecker chemistry. The supplied review describes the same C5 hydroxynitrile downstream of its proposed cyanosulfidic branch, but does not independently demonstrate this aldehyde-supply route. Its integration here is therefore speculative relative to the supplied evidence. The reaction preserves the isopropyl branch and introduces the future carboxyl carbon without any chain shortening.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Net conversion of the valine cyanohydrin reservoir to aminonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_015\",\n        \"mol_003\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_017\",\n        \"mol_001\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Ammonia-rich anoxic solution, approximately pH 8-10; sufficient cyanide activity and volatile retention are needed. The cyanohydrin, aldehyde, and imine pools interconvert.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The cyanohydrin equilibrates with aldehyde and HCN; ammonia forms an imine or iminium species, and cyanide capture yields the aminonitrile. The displayed reaction is the balanced net conversion, not direct displacement of an unactivated hydroxyl group.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. (2015), as summarized in the supplied review, motivates this cyanohydrin-to-aminonitrile connection. The review's simple hydroxyl-substitution explanation is replaced by the chemically appropriate Strecker equilibrium mechanism. This entry shares imine chemistry with rxn_009 and rxn_010, so the resulting graph paths must not be represented as independent reaction families. No enantioselection is supplied.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Isobutyraldehyde-ammonia imine formation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_014\",\n        \"mol_003\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_016\",\n        \"mol_001\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Ammonia-rich anoxic solution undergoing concentration; imine is a transient equilibrium species rather than an assumed isolable reservoir.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ammonia adds to the aldehyde to form a carbinolamine, followed by reversible dehydration to the imine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Imine formation is part of classical Strecker chemistry (Strecker, 1850), although that historical precedent does not establish this specific prebiotic setting. This explicit aldehyde-entry branch is not demonstrated in the supplied review and is speculative as an environmental pathway. Aqueous imine concentrations may be low; subsequent cyanide trapping, rather than imine isolation, must pull the sequence forward.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanide trapping of isobutyraldimine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_016\",\n        \"mol_002\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_017\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic imine/iminium-HCN equilibrium mixture with ammonia present; proton-transfer equilibria determine the reactive species.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyanide attacks the imine or iminium carbon, followed by proton transfer to give the alpha-aminonitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is the defining carbon-carbon bond-forming step of the classical Strecker reaction family (Strecker, 1850). Its proposed geochemical implementation remains speculative relative to the supplied evidence. The branched aldehyde provides the correct valine side chain, and cyanide supplies the eventual carboxyl carbon. Attack on either face produces a racemic precursor.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct aminonitrile hydrolysis to valine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_017\",\n        \"mol_001\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_020\",\n        \"mol_003\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"hydronium as a general-acid catalyst\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-370 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"hydronium\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Acidic volcanic-water setting, approximately pH 1-2, reached after aminonitrile formation; prolonged residence may be necessary. Ammonia and valine are substantially protonated under these conditions.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Sequential nitrile hydration to 2-amino-3-methylbutanamide and amide hydrolysis forms valine and releases the nitrile nitrogen as ammonia. Two water molecules are consumed per aminonitrile, with coefficients omitted from the graph as required.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is the standard Strecker hydrolysis connection described in the supplied review in association with Patel et al. (2015). It does not establish efficient hydrolysis in ordinary cool pools: residence time, acid availability, and competing degradation are important bottlenecks. The C5 aminonitrile gives C5 valine without carbon loss. The supplied C6 aminonitrile alternative is excluded because ordinary hydrolysis instead gives leucine.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"Carbonation and cyclization of valine aminonitrile to hydantoin\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_017\",\n        \"mol_006\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_018\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-360 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"approximately 1 atm, with sustained dissolved inorganic carbon supply\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic ammonia/ammonium-containing concentrated solution with CO2-bicarbonate-carbonate equilibria; approximately pH 8-10. Conditions are separate from strongly acidic nitrile hydrolysis.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Aminonitrile carbonation produces a carbamate-related intermediate, followed by intramolecular reaction at the nitrile and rearrangement to the hydantoin. Net incorporation of CO2 gives the balanced cyclic product without adding a third nitrogen.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The Bucherer-Bergs reaction family provides laboratory precedent for hydantoin formation from carbonyl/cyanide/ammonia/carbonate systems (Bucherer and Steiner, 1934). SPECULATIVE as a prebiotic extension: it is not documented in the supplied Patel et al. (2015) review, and environmental rates and yields for this aminonitrile are unestablished. It is included as a chemically distinct sequestration and release route, not as evidence that carbonate necessarily improves valine production.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_013\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydantoin ring opening and decarbamoylation to valine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_018\",\n        \"mol_001\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_020\",\n        \"mol_003\",\n        \"mol_006\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"hydroxide and hydronium in successive hydrolysis stages\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"400-450 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"20-100 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"general acid/base catalysis in separate stages\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic hot-water circulation; alkaline ring opening followed by conditions permitting cleavage of the N-carbamoyl group. Transport from the surface hydantoin-forming compartment and subsequent cooling are conditional requirements.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrolytic ring opening forms N-carbamoylvaline; subsequent ureido-group hydrolysis releases valine, ammonia, and CO2. Two water molecules are consumed overall. The expelled carbon is the extra carbonate-derived carbon, not a carbon removed from the valine skeleton.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Hydantoin hydrolysis is an established amino-acid preparation strategy, but this particular natural hydrothermal implementation is SPECULATIVE and unsupported by the supplied review. Hydantoins and N-carbamoyl amino acids can be persistent, so stronger conditions are assigned rather than assuming facile ambient hydrolysis. Natural pH sequences, residence times, transport, and competition with valine degradation require validation. No enzyme or chiral catalyst is assumed.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Acetone-cyanosulfidic entry with direct nitrile hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Acetone enters the reviewed cyanohydrin/thioamide branch, followed by the explicitly speculative C5-forming conversion, Strecker equilibration, and direct hydrolysis. Feasibility depends critically on rxn_003.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_001\",\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_011\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_011\",\n        \"mol_012\",\n        \"mol_015\",\n        \"mol_017\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Acetone-cyanosulfidic entry with hydantoin-mediated release\",\n      \"description\": \"The acetone branch supplies aminonitrile, which is carbonated and cyclized before hydrothermal release of valine. This route inherits the unresolved cyanosulfidic conversion and adds conditional hydantoin formation and hydrolysis.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_001\",\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_012\",\n        \"rxn_013\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_012\",\n        \"mol_015\",\n        \"mol_017\",\n        \"mol_018\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Aldol-derived aldehyde through cyanohydrin and direct hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Propanal and formaldehyde conditionally supply methacrolein, whose selective hydrogenation supplies isobutyraldehyde. Cyanohydrin formation, net ammoniation, and nitrile hydrolysis then yield valine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_004\",\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_011\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_013\",\n        \"mol_014\",\n        \"mol_015\",\n        \"mol_017\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"Aldol-derived aldehyde through cyanohydrin and hydantoin\",\n      \"description\": \"The conditional aldol/hydrogenation branch enters the cyanohydrin reservoir. Its aminonitrile product proceeds through hydantoin formation and staged hydrolysis rather than direct nitrile hydrolysis.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_004\",\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_012\",\n        \"rxn_013\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_013\",\n        \"mol_014\",\n        \"mol_015\",\n        \"mol_017\",\n        \"mol_018\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"Aldol-derived aldehyde through explicit imine capture\",\n      \"description\": \"Aldol-derived isobutyraldehyde reacts first with ammonia. Cyanide traps the transient imine, and direct aminonitrile hydrolysis yields valine. This is an alternative Strecker entry sequence, not a separate Strecker mechanism.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_004\",\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_010\",\n        \"rxn_011\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_013\",\n        \"mol_014\",\n        \"mol_016\",\n        \"mol_017\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"Aldol-derived imine capture with hydantoin-mediated release\",\n      \"description\": \"The conditional aldol branch supplies aldehyde for imine-first Strecker chemistry. The resulting aminonitrile is routed through hydantoin rather than directly hydrolyzed.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_004\",\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_010\",\n        \"rxn_012\",\n        \"rxn_013\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_014\",\n        \"mol_016\",\n        \"mol_017\",\n        \"mol_018\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"Alcohol-derived aldehyde through cyanohydrin and direct hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Conditional anaerobic isobutanol dehydrogenation supplies isobutyraldehyde. Cyanohydrin equilibration and ammoniation provide the aminonitrile for direct hydrolysis. A sufficiently H2-poor aldehyde-producing environment is essential.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_011\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_014\",\n        \"mol_015\",\n        \"mol_017\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"Alcohol-derived cyanohydrin with hydantoin-mediated release\",\n      \"description\": \"The speculative alcohol-supply branch feeds the cyanohydrin entry to Strecker chemistry. Carbonation and cyclization divert its aminonitrile through hydantoin before valine release.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_012\",\n        \"rxn_013\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_014\",\n        \"mol_015\",\n        \"mol_017\",\n        \"mol_018\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"Alcohol-derived aldehyde through imine and direct hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Isobutanol conditionally supplies the branched aldehyde, which enters imine-first Strecker chemistry. Direct hydrolysis of the resulting C5 aminonitrile yields racemic valine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_010\",\n        \"rxn_011\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_014\",\n        \"mol_016\",\n        \"mol_017\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"name\": \"Alcohol-derived imine capture with hydantoin-mediated release\",\n      \"description\": \"Conditional alcohol dehydrogenation is followed by imine formation and cyanide capture. Hydantoin formation and staged hydrolysis provide the alternative target-release sequence.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_010\",\n        \"rxn_012\",\n        \"rxn_013\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_014\",\n        \"mol_016\",\n        \"mol_017\",\n        \"mol_018\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\n    \"mol_001\",\n    \"mol_002\",\n    \"mol_003\",\n    \"mol_004\",\n    \"mol_014\",\n    \"mol_015\",\n    \"mol_017\",\n    \"mol_020\"\n  ],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_004\",\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_009\"\n      ],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_011\",\n        \"rxn_013\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_003\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_011\",\n        \"rxn_013\"\n      ],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_009\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_014\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_006\"\n      ],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_009\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_015\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_007\"\n      ],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_008\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_017\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_010\"\n      ],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_011\",\n        \"rxn_012\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_020\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_011\",\n        \"rxn_013\"\n      ],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": []\n    }\n  ]\n}", "parsed_response": {"target_molecule": "Valine (racemic)", "overall_strategy": "Construct an atom-balanced, conditional network with ten distinct reaction-node sequences, not ten independently demonstrated prebiotic syntheses. Three carbon-feedstock branches converge on valine precursors: acetone-derived cyanosulfidic chemistry, propanal/formaldehyde aldol chemistry, and isobutanol dehydrogenation. Aldehyde-derived branches can enter Strecker chemistry through cyanohydrin or imine intermediates; these are alternative entry sequences within the same reaction family, not independent mechanisms. Aminonitriles subsequently undergo either direct hydrolysis or hydantoin formation followed by hydrolysis. The ten listed pathways comprise two acetone-derived routes, four aldol-derived routes, and four alcohol-derived routes. Unsupported prebiotic applications and the particularly uncertain cyanosulfidic chain-extension step are explicitly marked speculative. The supplied review is not accepted uncritically: its C6 aminonitrile would hydrolyze to leucine, not valine; its chain-extension equation omits nitrogen-containing products; and its achiral chemistry does not establish selective L-valine production. Those errors are not propagated. Organic feedstock availability remains a boundary assumption. Reactions require separate compatible environments rather than a single mixed reactor. Neutral molecular formulas represent connectivity, with protonation controlled by local pH. Hydrolysis nodes explicitly describe lumped amide intermediates. A null environment_formed denotes either an externally supplied feedstock or, for valine, formation in more than one environment.", "molecules": [{"id": "mol_001", "formula": "H2O", "iupac_name": "oxidane", "common_name": "water", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_002", "formula": "CHN", "iupac_name": "hydrogen cyanide", "common_name": "hydrogen cyanide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_003", "formula": "NH3", "iupac_name": "azane", "common_name": "ammonia", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_004", "formula": "H2S", "iupac_name": "sulfane", "common_name": "hydrogen sulfide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2S/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_005", "formula": "H2", "iupac_name": "dihydrogen", "common_name": "hydrogen", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_006", "formula": "CO2", "iupac_name": "carbon dioxide", "common_name": "carbon dioxide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CO2/c2-1-3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_007", "formula": "C3H6O", "iupac_name": "propan-2-one", "common_name": "acetone", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H6O/c1-3(2)4/h1-2H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_008", "formula": "C3H6O", "iupac_name": "propanal", "common_name": "propionaldehyde", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H6O/c1-2-3-4/h3H,2H2,1H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_009", "formula": "CH2O", "iupac_name": "methanal", "common_name": "formaldehyde", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CH2O/c1-2/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_010", "formula": "C4H10O", "iupac_name": "2-methylpropan-1-ol", "common_name": "isobutanol", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C4H10O/c1-4(2)3-5/h4-5H,3H2,1-2H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_011", "formula": "C4H7NO", "iupac_name": "2-hydroxy-2-methylpropanenitrile", "common_name": "acetone cyanohydrin", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C4H7NO/c1-4(2,6)3-5/h6H,1-2H3", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_012", "formula": "C4H9NOS", "iupac_name": "2-hydroxy-2-methylpropanethioamide", "common_name": null, "inchi": "InChI=1S/C4H9NOS/c1-4(2,6)3(5)7/h6H,1-2H3,(H2,5,7)", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_013", "formula": "C4H6O", "iupac_name": "2-methylprop-2-enal", "common_name": "methacrolein", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_014", "formula": "C4H8O", "iupac_name": "2-methylpropanal", "common_name": "isobutyraldehyde", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C4H8O/c1-4(2)3-5/h3-4H,1-2H3", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_015", "formula": "C5H9NO", "iupac_name": "2-hydroxy-3-methylbutanenitrile", "common_name": "isobutyraldehyde cyanohydrin", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C5H9NO/c1-4(2)5(7)3-6/h4-5,7H,1-2H3", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_016", "formula": "C4H9N", "iupac_name": "2-methylpropan-1-imine", "common_name": "isobutyraldimine", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_017", "formula": "C5H10N2", "iupac_name": "2-amino-3-methylbutanenitrile", "common_name": "valine aminonitrile", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C5H10N2/c1-4(2)5(7)3-6/h4-5H,7H2,1-2H3", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_018", "formula": "C6H10N2O2", "iupac_name": "5-(propan-2-yl)imidazolidine-2,4-dione", "common_name": "5-isopropylhydantoin", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_019", "formula": "S8", "iupac_name": "cyclooctasulfur", "common_name": "elemental sulfur", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_020", "formula": "C5H11NO2", "iupac_name": "2-amino-3-methylbutanoic acid", "common_name": "valine, racemic", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C5H11NO2/c1-3(2)4(6)5(7)8/h3-4H,6H2,1-2H3,(H,7,8)", "role": "target", "environment_formed": null}], "reactions": [{"id": "rxn_001", "name": "Acetone cyanohydrin formation", "inputs": ["mol_007", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_011"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320 K", "pressure": "approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic aqueous pool; mildly alkaline conditions provide some cyanide while retaining HCN. Concentration and protection from rapid volatilization are required; UV is not required for this addition."}, "mechanism": "Reversible cyanide addition to the acetone carbonyl followed by protonation produces the tertiary cyanohydrin.", "reasoning": "This is chemically conventional cyanohydrin chemistry and is the acetone-entry reaction attributed to Patel et al. (2015) in the supplied review. No mineral is needed. Equilibrium, HCN loss, and competing cyanide chemistry limit accumulation; the review does not establish a general environmental yield."}, {"id": "rxn_002", "name": "Sulfide addition to acetone cyanohydrin nitrile", "inputs": ["mol_011", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_012"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K", "pressure": "approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic sulfide-containing solution, approximately pH 8-9; H2S and HS- are represented by their neutral parent species."}, "mechanism": "Hydrosulfide attacks the nitrile carbon; proton transfer and tautomerization generate the thioamide.", "reasoning": "The supplied review attributes this nitrile-to-thioamide transformation to Patel et al. (2015). H2S is a consumed reagent, not an unexplained catalytic agent. Cyanohydrin dissociation and competing sulfide reactions remain relevant; substrate-specific rates and yields are not independently verified here."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Hypothetical cyanosulfidic deoxygenative homologation and cyanide trapping", "inputs": ["mol_012", "mol_002", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_015", "mol_003", "mol_019"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Cu(I)-cyanide photoredox complexes, proposed for this specific transformation"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320 K", "pressure": "approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Cu(I)-cyanide photoredox complexes", "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic UV-irradiated cyanide/sulfide solution; staged chemistry rather than an assumed one-pot process. Copper speciation, sulfide precipitation, and sulfur-product speciation require experimental determination."}, "mechanism": "Unresolved net sequence requiring removal of the tertiary hydroxyl functionality, conversion of the original thioamide carbon into an aldehyde-equivalent carbon, loss of the original nitrogen as ammonia, and capture by a new cyanide. Elemental sulfur is an idealized terminal oxidation sink; transient polysulfides are not resolved.", "reasoning": "SPECULATIVE: the supplied report attributes a related transformation to Patel et al. (2015), but its displayed equation loses nitrogen and cannot establish the proposed mechanism. This replacement is atom-balanced with inferred coefficients and explicitly includes ammonia, but balancing does not demonstrate feasibility. The first cyanide-derived carbon becomes the eventual valine alpha carbon; the second becomes its carboxyl carbon. Cu(I) photoredox catalysis is chemically distinct from claiming dissolved elemental copper. Aldehyde formation, productive reduction, sulfur products, and selectivity must be demonstrated. This is the decisive unresolved bottleneck for P1 and P2."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Crossed aldol addition and dehydration of propanal with formaldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_008", "mol_009"], "outputs": ["mol_013", "mol_001"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["hydroxide as a general-base catalyst"], "conditions": {"temperature": "315-350 K", "pressure": "approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "hydroxide", "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic alkaline pool, approximately pH 9-11; concentration cycles may favor condensation. Mixed-product formation is expected rather than selective methacrolein production."}, "mechanism": "Propanal enolate adds to formaldehyde, producing a hydroxymethylated aldehyde that dehydrates to methacrolein. This node represents the net aldol/dehydration sequence.", "reasoning": "SPECULATIVE as a prebiotic feedstock-supply reaction; it is not supported by the supplied Patel et al. (2015) review. Standard crossed-aldol chemistry provides a carbon-conserving way to construct the required branched C4 skeleton. Propanal self-condensation, formaldehyde disproportionation, repeated hydroxymethylation, and polymerization compete. Selectivity under the stated geochemical conditions is unestablished."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Candidate selective hydrogenation of methacrolein to isobutyraldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_013", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_014"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["native Ni-Fe alloy surface, such as awaruite, as a candidate hydrogenation catalyst"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-420 K", "pressure": "10-100 bar, with sufficient H2 activity", "catalyst": "native Ni-Fe alloy", "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic, locally sulfide-poor rock-water interface with accessible metallic sites; effluent must cool before downstream cyanide chemistry."}, "mechanism": "Surface activation of hydrogen followed by addition across the carbon-carbon double bond would preserve the aldehyde group and produce isobutyraldehyde.", "reasoning": "SPECULATIVE: no substrate-specific prebiotic demonstration is supplied. Ni-containing metallic surfaces are chosen for hydrogen activation and alkene hydrogenation, not merely because they are minerals. The crucial unresolved issue is selectivity: carbonyl reduction, full conversion to alcohols, hydrogenolysis, and surface poisoning may prevent aldehyde accumulation. This bottleneck conditions all aldol-derived pathways."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Candidate isobutanol dehydrogenation to isobutyraldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_010"], "outputs": ["mol_014", "mol_005"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["native copper surface as a candidate heterogeneous dehydrogenation catalyst"], "conditions": {"temperature": "450-520 K", "pressure": "50-150 bar", "catalyst": "heterogeneous Cu(0)", "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic, sulfide-poor, H2-poor flowing hydrothermal zone; product removal and rapid effluent cooling are required. This is not assigned to an H2-rich serpentinization reactor."}, "mechanism": "Catalytic removal of hydrogen from the alcohol O-H bond and adjacent C-H bond forms the aldehyde and molecular hydrogen without molecular oxygen.", "reasoning": "SPECULATIVE geochemical application of an established alcohol-dehydrogenation reaction family; the supplied research does not document this reaction. Copper here is a solid catalyst, not dissolved Cu(0) or an unspecified stoichiometric reductant. H2-rich conditions oppose dehydrogenation, while sulfide can deactivate copper. Adequate isobutanol supply, reaction rate in water, and aldehyde survival remain unresolved."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Isobutyraldehyde cyanohydrin formation", "inputs": ["mol_014", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_015"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320 K", "pressure": "approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic concentrated solution, approximately pH 7-9; retention of volatile aldehyde and HCN is necessary. Shaded pore water can limit photochemical destruction between irradiation episodes."}, "mechanism": "Reversible cyanide addition to isobutyraldehyde followed by protonation gives the C5 alpha-hydroxynitrile.", "reasoning": "This is a conventional cyanohydrin entry to Strecker chemistry. The supplied review describes the same C5 hydroxynitrile downstream of its proposed cyanosulfidic branch, but does not independently demonstrate this aldehyde-supply route. Its integration here is therefore speculative relative to the supplied evidence. The reaction preserves the isopropyl branch and introduces the future carboxyl carbon without any chain shortening."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Net conversion of the valine cyanohydrin reservoir to aminonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_015", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_017", "mol_001"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K", "pressure": "approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Ammonia-rich anoxic solution, approximately pH 8-10; sufficient cyanide activity and volatile retention are needed. The cyanohydrin, aldehyde, and imine pools interconvert."}, "mechanism": "The cyanohydrin equilibrates with aldehyde and HCN; ammonia forms an imine or iminium species, and cyanide capture yields the aminonitrile. The displayed reaction is the balanced net conversion, not direct displacement of an unactivated hydroxyl group.", "reasoning": "Patel et al. (2015), as summarized in the supplied review, motivates this cyanohydrin-to-aminonitrile connection. The review's simple hydroxyl-substitution explanation is replaced by the chemically appropriate Strecker equilibrium mechanism. This entry shares imine chemistry with rxn_009 and rxn_010, so the resulting graph paths must not be represented as independent reaction families. No enantioselection is supplied."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "Isobutyraldehyde-ammonia imine formation", "inputs": ["mol_014", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_016", "mol_001"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K", "pressure": "approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Ammonia-rich anoxic solution undergoing concentration; imine is a transient equilibrium species rather than an assumed isolable reservoir."}, "mechanism": "Ammonia adds to the aldehyde to form a carbinolamine, followed by reversible dehydration to the imine.", "reasoning": "Imine formation is part of classical Strecker chemistry (Strecker, 1850), although that historical precedent does not establish this specific prebiotic setting. This explicit aldehyde-entry branch is not demonstrated in the supplied review and is speculative as an environmental pathway. Aqueous imine concentrations may be low; subsequent cyanide trapping, rather than imine isolation, must pull the sequence forward."}, {"id": "rxn_010", "name": "Cyanide trapping of isobutyraldimine", "inputs": ["mol_016", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_017"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K", "pressure": "approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic imine/iminium-HCN equilibrium mixture with ammonia present; proton-transfer equilibria determine the reactive species."}, "mechanism": "Cyanide attacks the imine or iminium carbon, followed by proton transfer to give the alpha-aminonitrile.", "reasoning": "This is the defining carbon-carbon bond-forming step of the classical Strecker reaction family (Strecker, 1850). Its proposed geochemical implementation remains speculative relative to the supplied evidence. The branched aldehyde provides the correct valine side chain, and cyanide supplies the eventual carboxyl carbon. Attack on either face produces a racemic precursor."}, {"id": "rxn_011", "name": "Direct aminonitrile hydrolysis to valine", "inputs": ["mol_017", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_020", "mol_003"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["hydronium as a general-acid catalyst"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-370 K", "pressure": "approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "hydronium", "aqueous": true, "other": "Acidic volcanic-water setting, approximately pH 1-2, reached after aminonitrile formation; prolonged residence may be necessary. Ammonia and valine are substantially protonated under these conditions."}, "mechanism": "Sequential nitrile hydration to 2-amino-3-methylbutanamide and amide hydrolysis forms valine and releases the nitrile nitrogen as ammonia. Two water molecules are consumed per aminonitrile, with coefficients omitted from the graph as required.", "reasoning": "This is the standard Strecker hydrolysis connection described in the supplied review in association with Patel et al. (2015). It does not establish efficient hydrolysis in ordinary cool pools: residence time, acid availability, and competing degradation are important bottlenecks. The C5 aminonitrile gives C5 valine without carbon loss. The supplied C6 aminonitrile alternative is excluded because ordinary hydrolysis instead gives leucine."}, {"id": "rxn_012", "name": "Carbonation and cyclization of valine aminonitrile to hydantoin", "inputs": ["mol_017", "mol_006"], "outputs": ["mol_018"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "320-360 K", "pressure": "approximately 1 atm, with sustained dissolved inorganic carbon supply", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic ammonia/ammonium-containing concentrated solution with CO2-bicarbonate-carbonate equilibria; approximately pH 8-10. Conditions are separate from strongly acidic nitrile hydrolysis."}, "mechanism": "Aminonitrile carbonation produces a carbamate-related intermediate, followed by intramolecular reaction at the nitrile and rearrangement to the hydantoin. Net incorporation of CO2 gives the balanced cyclic product without adding a third nitrogen.", "reasoning": "The Bucherer-Bergs reaction family provides laboratory precedent for hydantoin formation from carbonyl/cyanide/ammonia/carbonate systems (Bucherer and Steiner, 1934). SPECULATIVE as a prebiotic extension: it is not documented in the supplied Patel et al. (2015) review, and environmental rates and yields for this aminonitrile are unestablished. It is included as a chemically distinct sequestration and release route, not as evidence that carbonate necessarily improves valine production."}, {"id": "rxn_013", "name": "Hydantoin ring opening and decarbamoylation to valine", "inputs": ["mol_018", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_020", "mol_003", "mol_006"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["hydroxide and hydronium in successive hydrolysis stages"], "conditions": {"temperature": "400-450 K", "pressure": "20-100 bar", "catalyst": "general acid/base catalysis in separate stages", "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic hot-water circulation; alkaline ring opening followed by conditions permitting cleavage of the N-carbamoyl group. Transport from the surface hydantoin-forming compartment and subsequent cooling are conditional requirements."}, "mechanism": "Hydrolytic ring opening forms N-carbamoylvaline; subsequent ureido-group hydrolysis releases valine, ammonia, and CO2. Two water molecules are consumed overall. The expelled carbon is the extra carbonate-derived carbon, not a carbon removed from the valine skeleton.", "reasoning": "Hydantoin hydrolysis is an established amino-acid preparation strategy, but this particular natural hydrothermal implementation is SPECULATIVE and unsupported by the supplied review. Hydantoins and N-carbamoyl amino acids can be persistent, so stronger conditions are assigned rather than assuming facile ambient hydrolysis. Natural pH sequences, residence times, transport, and competition with valine degradation require validation. No enzyme or chiral catalyst is assumed."}], "pathways": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "name": "Acetone-cyanosulfidic entry with direct nitrile hydrolysis", "description": "Acetone enters the reviewed cyanohydrin/thioamide branch, followed by the explicitly speculative C5-forming conversion, Strecker equilibration, and direct hydrolysis. Feasibility depends critically on rxn_003.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_008", "rxn_011"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_011", "mol_012", "mol_015", "mol_017"]}, {"pathway_id": "P2", "name": "Acetone-cyanosulfidic entry with hydantoin-mediated release", "description": "The acetone branch supplies aminonitrile, which is carbonated and cyclized before hydrothermal release of valine. This route inherits the unresolved cyanosulfidic conversion and adds conditional hydantoin formation and hydrolysis.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_008", "rxn_012", "rxn_013"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_012", "mol_015", "mol_017", "mol_018"]}, {"pathway_id": "P3", "name": "Aldol-derived aldehyde through cyanohydrin and direct hydrolysis", "description": "Propanal and formaldehyde conditionally supply methacrolein, whose selective hydrogenation supplies isobutyraldehyde. Cyanohydrin formation, net ammoniation, and nitrile hydrolysis then yield valine.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_004", "rxn_005", "rxn_007", "rxn_008", "rxn_011"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_013", "mol_014", "mol_015", "mol_017"]}, {"pathway_id": "P4", "name": "Aldol-derived aldehyde through cyanohydrin and hydantoin", "description": "The conditional aldol/hydrogenation branch enters the cyanohydrin reservoir. Its aminonitrile product proceeds through hydantoin formation and staged hydrolysis rather than direct nitrile hydrolysis.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_004", "rxn_005", "rxn_007", "rxn_008", "rxn_012", "rxn_013"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_013", "mol_014", "mol_015", "mol_017", "mol_018"]}, {"pathway_id": "P5", "name": "Aldol-derived aldehyde through explicit imine capture", "description": "Aldol-derived isobutyraldehyde reacts first with ammonia. Cyanide traps the transient imine, and direct aminonitrile hydrolysis yields valine. This is an alternative Strecker entry sequence, not a separate Strecker mechanism.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_004", "rxn_005", "rxn_009", "rxn_010", "rxn_011"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_013", "mol_014", "mol_016", "mol_017"]}, {"pathway_id": "P6", "name": "Aldol-derived imine capture with hydantoin-mediated release", "description": "The conditional aldol branch supplies aldehyde for imine-first Strecker chemistry. The resulting aminonitrile is routed through hydantoin rather than directly hydrolyzed.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_004", "rxn_005", "rxn_009", "rxn_010", "rxn_012", "rxn_013"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_014", "mol_016", "mol_017", "mol_018"]}, {"pathway_id": "P7", "name": "Alcohol-derived aldehyde through cyanohydrin and direct hydrolysis", "description": "Conditional anaerobic isobutanol dehydrogenation supplies isobutyraldehyde. Cyanohydrin equilibration and ammoniation provide the aminonitrile for direct hydrolysis. A sufficiently H2-poor aldehyde-producing environment is essential.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_006", "rxn_007", "rxn_008", "rxn_011"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_014", "mol_015", "mol_017"]}, {"pathway_id": "P8", "name": "Alcohol-derived cyanohydrin with hydantoin-mediated release", "description": "The speculative alcohol-supply branch feeds the cyanohydrin entry to Strecker chemistry. Carbonation and cyclization divert its aminonitrile through hydantoin before valine release.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_006", "rxn_007", "rxn_008", "rxn_012", "rxn_013"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_014", "mol_015", "mol_017", "mol_018"]}, {"pathway_id": "P9", "name": "Alcohol-derived aldehyde through imine and direct hydrolysis", "description": "Isobutanol conditionally supplies the branched aldehyde, which enters imine-first Strecker chemistry. Direct hydrolysis of the resulting C5 aminonitrile yields racemic valine.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_006", "rxn_009", "rxn_010", "rxn_011"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_014", "mol_016", "mol_017"]}, {"pathway_id": "P10", "name": "Alcohol-derived imine capture with hydantoin-mediated release", "description": "Conditional alcohol dehydrogenation is followed by imine formation and cyanide capture. Hydantoin formation and staged hydrolysis provide the alternative target-release sequence.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_006", "rxn_009", "rxn_010", "rxn_012", "rxn_013"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_014", "mol_016", "mol_017", "mol_018"]}], "hub_molecules": ["mol_001", "mol_002", "mol_003", "mol_004", "mol_014", "mol_015", "mol_017", "mol_020"], "convergence_points": [{"molecule_id": "mol_001", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_004", "rxn_008", "rxn_009"], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_011", "rxn_013"]}, {"molecule_id": "mol_003", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_003", "rxn_011", "rxn_013"], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_008", "rxn_009"]}, {"molecule_id": "mol_014", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_005", "rxn_006"], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_007", "rxn_009"]}, {"molecule_id": "mol_015", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_003", "rxn_007"], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_008"]}, {"molecule_id": "mol_017", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_008", "rxn_010"], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_011", "rxn_012"]}, {"molecule_id": "mol_020", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_011", "rxn_013"], "outgoing_reactions": []}]}, "reasoning": "", "duration_ms": 476013.1630897522, "success": true, "error_message": null}
{"timestamp": "2026-10-19T17:22:15.049762", "agent_name": "CriticAgent", "method_name": "evaluate_network", "step": null, "attempt": null, "system_message": "You are evaluating a COMPLETE SYNTHESIS NETWORK, not individual reactions.\n\n## Neutrality\n- Do not reward or penalize a network for using (or not using) any particular feedstock, intermediate,\n  reaction family, or prebiotic framework. Judge only the evidence and the chemistry.\n- The number of shared/hub molecules, the number of convergence points, and the order or number of\n  environments used are NOT scoring criteria.\n\n## Evaluation Criteria\n\n### 1. Network Validity (0.0-1.0)\n- Are there sufficient molecule and reaction nodes?\n- Is the network connected (no isolated subgraphs)?\n- Are molecule IDs used consistently, and are reactions approximately balanced?\n- When a DETERMINISTIC ATOM/CHARGE BALANCE CHECK section is provided, treat it as ground truth for\n  mass and charge conservation; reactions it reports as UNBALANCED are conservation violations.\n\n### 2. Chemical Feasibility (0.0-1.0)\n- Is each reaction chemically sound?\n- Are the conditions appropriate for each environment?\n- Are the mechanisms plausible?\n\n### 3. Environmental Consistency (0.0-1.0)\n- Are each reaction's conditions (temperature, pressure, pH, catalysts, energy source) consistent with its stated environment?\n- If molecules are transferred between environments, is the transfer physically justified?\n- If a catalyst or mineral agent is cited, is it realistic and present in that environment?\n\n### 4. Pathway Coherence (0.0-1.0)\n- Do pathways form valid routes to the target?\n- Are key intermediates properly identified?\n- Is the overall strategy sound?\n\n### 5. Target Achievement (0.0-1.0)\n- Does the network actually produce the target molecule?\n- Are the final steps chemically valid?\n\n### 6. Literature Alignment (0.0-1.0) - When research findings are provided\n- Are reactions supported by experimental evidence from cited studies?\n- Are literature citations present in reaction reasoning?\n- Does the network acknowledge critiques and known challenges identified in the research?\n- Departures from the research are acceptable when explicitly and soundly justified.\n\n## Scoring Guidelines\n- 0.9-1.0: Excellent network, valid and chemically sound\n- 0.8-0.9: Good network with minor issues\n- 0.7-0.8: Acceptable but with notable concerns\n- 0.5-0.7: Significant problems with validity or chemistry\n- 0.0-0.5: Major issues, network is not viable\n\nA network is APPROVED if overall_confidence >= 0.9\n\nOutput your evaluation as JSON matching the NetworkEvaluation schema.\n", "user_message": "Evaluate this synthesis network:\n\nTARGET MOLECULE: Valine (racemic)\nOVERALL STRATEGY: Construct an atom-balanced, conditional network with ten distinct reaction-node sequences, not ten independently demonstrated prebiotic syntheses. Three carbon-feedstock branches converge on valine precursors: acetone-derived cyanosulfidic chemistry, propanal/formaldehyde aldol chemistry, and isobutanol dehydrogenation. Aldehyde-derived branches can enter Strecker chemistry through cyanohydrin or imine intermediates; these are alternative entry sequences within the same reaction family, not independent mechanisms. Aminonitriles subsequently undergo either direct hydrolysis or hydantoin formation followed by hydrolysis. The ten listed pathways comprise two acetone-derived routes, four aldol-derived routes, and four alcohol-derived routes. Unsupported prebiotic applications and the particularly uncertain cyanosulfidic chain-extension step are explicitly marked speculative. The supplied review is not accepted uncritically: its C6 aminonitrile would hydrolyze to leucine, not valine; its chain-extension equation omits nitrogen-containing products; and its achiral chemistry does not establish selective L-valine production. Those errors are not propagated. Organic feedstock availability remains a boundary assumption. Reactions require separate compatible environments rather than a single mixed reactor. Neutral molecular formulas represent connectivity, with protonation controlled by local pH. Hydrolysis nodes explicitly describe lumped amide intermediates. A null environment_formed denotes either an externally supplied feedstock or, for valine, formation in more than one environment.\n\nMOLECULES (20 nodes):\n  - mol_001: H2O (water) [starting]\n  - mol_002: CHN (hydrogen cyanide) [starting]\n  - mol_003: NH3 (ammonia) [starting]\n  - mol_004: H2S (hydrogen sulfide) [starting]\n  - mol_005: H2 (hydrogen) [starting]\n  - mol_006: CO2 (carbon dioxide) [starting]\n  - mol_007: C3H6O (acetone) [starting]\n  - mol_008: C3H6O (propionaldehyde) [starting]\n  - mol_009: CH2O (formaldehyde) [starting]\n  - mol_010: C4H10O (isobutanol) [starting]\n  - mol_011: C4H7NO (acetone cyanohydrin) [intermediate]\n  - mol_012: C4H9NOS (2-hydroxy-2-methylpropanethioamide) [intermediate]\n  - mol_013: C4H6O (methacrolein) [intermediate]\n  - mol_014: C4H8O (isobutyraldehyde) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_015: C5H9NO (isobutyraldehyde cyanohydrin) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_016: C4H9N (isobutyraldimine) [intermediate]\n  - mol_017: C5H10N2 (valine aminonitrile) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_018: C6H10N2O2 (5-isopropylhydantoin) [intermediate]\n  - mol_019: S8 (elemental sulfur) [intermediate]\n  - mol_020: C5H11NO2 (valine, racemic) [target]\n\nREACTIONS (13 nodes):\n  - rxn_001: mol_007, mol_002 -> mol_011 (Acetone cyanohydrin formation) [Surface]\n  - rxn_002: mol_011, mol_004 -> mol_012 (Sulfide addition to acetone cyanohydrin nitrile) [Surface]\n  - rxn_003: mol_012, mol_002, mol_004 -> mol_015, mol_003, mol_019 (Hypothetical cyanosulfidic deoxygenative homologation and cyanide trapping) [Surface]\n  - rxn_004: mol_008, mol_009 -> mol_013, mol_001 (Crossed aldol addition and dehydration of propanal with formaldehyde) [Surface]\n  - rxn_005: mol_013, mol_005 -> mol_014 (Candidate selective hydrogenation of methacrolein to isobutyraldehyde) [Hydrothermal]\n  - rxn_006: mol_010 -> mol_014, mol_005 (Candidate isobutanol dehydrogenation to isobutyraldehyde) [Hydrothermal]\n  - rxn_007: mol_014, mol_002 -> mol_015 (Isobutyraldehyde cyanohydrin formation) [Surface]\n  - rxn_008: mol_015, mol_003 -> mol_017, mol_001 (Net conversion of the valine cyanohydrin reservoir to aminonitrile) [Surface]\n  - rxn_009: mol_014, mol_003 -> mol_016, mol_001 (Isobutyraldehyde-ammonia imine formation) [Surface]\n  - rxn_010: mol_016, mol_002 -> mol_017 (Cyanide trapping of isobutyraldimine) [Surface]\n  - rxn_011: mol_017, mol_001 -> mol_020, mol_003 (Direct aminonitrile hydrolysis to valine) [Surface]\n  - rxn_012: mol_017, mol_006 -> mol_018 (Carbonation and cyclization of valine aminonitrile to hydantoin) [Surface]\n  - rxn_013: mol_018, mol_001 -> mol_020, mol_003, mol_006 (Hydantoin ring opening and decarbamoylation to valine) [Hydrothermal]\n\nPATHWAYS (10):\n  - P1: Acetone-cyanosulfidic entry with direct nitrile hydrolysis (5 steps)\n  - P2: Acetone-cyanosulfidic entry with hydantoin-mediated release (6 steps)\n  - P3: Aldol-derived aldehyde through cyanohydrin and direct hydrolysis (5 steps)\n  - P4: Aldol-derived aldehyde through cyanohydrin and hydantoin (6 steps)\n  - P5: Aldol-derived aldehyde through explicit imine capture (5 steps)\n  - P6: Aldol-derived imine capture with hydantoin-mediated release (6 steps)\n  - P7: Alcohol-derived aldehyde through cyanohydrin and direct hydrolysis (4 steps)\n  - P8: Alcohol-derived cyanohydrin with hydantoin-mediated release (5 steps)\n  - P9: Alcohol-derived aldehyde through imine and direct hydrolysis (4 steps)\n  - P10: Alcohol-derived imine capture with hydantoin-mediated release (5 steps)\n\n\n=== DETERMINISTIC ATOM/CHARGE BALANCE CHECK (RDKit) ===\nComputed programmatically from each molecule's InChI (via RDKit) or stated formula. Reactions list no coefficients, so a reaction counts as balanced if some positive integer coefficients (<= 24) conserve every element and net charge.\nSummary: 13 reactions | 10 balanced as written | 3 balanceable with coefficients | 0 UNBALANCED | 0 unchecked\n  - rxn_003 balanceable: 4 C4H9NOS + 4 CHN + 4 H2S -> 4 C5H9NO + 4 NH3 + S8\n  - rxn_011 balanceable: C5H10N2 + 2 H2O -> C5H11NO2 + NH3\n  - rxn_013 balanceable: C6H10N2O2 + 2 H2O -> C5H11NO2 + NH3 + CO2\nTreat this check as ground truth for mass and charge conservation. Reactions that cannot be balanced violate conservation: reflect this in chemical_feasibility and reaction_scores, list them in problematic_reactions, and name the specific reaction IDs (with the missing species, if hinted) in your feedback so the network can be corrected. Note that implausibly large inferred coefficients may also indicate a mis-specified reaction.\n=== END BALANCE CHECK ===\n\n\n=== LITERATURE RESEARCH ON TARGET MOLECULE ===\nThe following research was conducted on the target molecule before network generation. Use it to assess whether the network aligns with known prebiotic chemistry literature.\n\n=== CURATED LITERATURE REVIEW ===\n# Prebiotic Synthesis of L-Valine: Comprehensive Literature Review\n\n## 1. Known Prebiotic Synthesis Routes\n\nTwo experimentally demonstrated pathways produce L-valine (C5H11NO2, SMILES: CC(C)C(C(=O)O)N, InChI: InChI=1S/C5H11NO2/c1-3(2)4(6)5(7)8/h3-4H,6H2,1-2H3,(H,7,8)) from prebiotically available precursors. Both operate within the cyanosulfidic protometabolism framework under surface environment conditions.\n\n### Pathway 1: Cyanosulfidic Protometabolism via Dihydroxyacetone (Surface Environment)\n\nThe cyanosulfidic protometabolism pathway demonstrated by Patel, Percivalle, Ritson, Duffy, and Sutherland (2015, *Nature Chemistry*, DOI: 10.1038/nchem.2202) constructs L-valine through a 6-step sequence starting from dihydroxyacetone, proceeding through acetone and a series of nitrile intermediates. This pathway follows the general cyanosulfidic strategy: sugar-derived ketones undergo cyanohydrin formation, thioamide conversion, reductive rearrangement with copper/H2S, amination, and final nitrile hydrolysis to the amino acid.\n\n**Step 1**: Dihydroxyacetone \u2192 Acetone + 2 H2O (Reductive deoxygenation)\n- SMILES: O=C(CO)CO>>CC(C)=O.O.O\n- Reactants: Dihydroxyacetone (C3H6O3, SMILES: C(C(=O)CO)O, InChI: InChI=1S/C3H6O3/c4-1-3(6)2-5/h4-5H,1-2H2, MW: 90.08)\n- Products: Acetone (C3H6O, SMILES: CC(=O)C, InChI: InChI=1S/C3H6O/c1-3(2)4/h1-2H3, MW: 58.08) + H2O\n- Environment: Surface (cyanosulfidic conditions)\n- Agents: **Sulfidic acid** (H2S, SMILES: S) and **Elemental copper** ([Cu]) as solutes\n- Mechanism: Reductive deoxygenation of the triose sugar dihydroxyacetone (DHA) under cyanosulfidic conditions. Copper acts as a single-electron reductant in concert with hydrogen sulfide, stripping the hydroxymethyl groups and reducing the C3 backbone to acetone. This converts a sugar feedstock into a simple ketone suitable for downstream cyanohydrin chemistry. The reaction is analogous to the broader Patel et al. demonstration that cyanosulfidic chemistry can interconvert sugars and metabolic intermediates.\n\n**Step 2**: Acetone + Hydrogen cyanide \u2192 Acetone cyanohydrin (Cyanohydrin formation)\n- SMILES: CC(C)=O.C#N>>CC(C)(O)C#N\n- Reactants: Acetone (C3H6O, SMILES: CC(=O)C) + Hydrogen cyanide (CHN, SMILES: C#N, InChI: InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H, MW: 27.03)\n- Products: Acetone cyanohydrin (C4H7NO, SMILES: CC(C)(C#N)O, InChI: InChI=1S/C4H7NO/c1-4(2,6)3-5/h6H,1-2H3, MW: 85.10)\n- Environment: Surface (cyanosulfidic conditions)\n- Mechanism: Nucleophilic addition of cyanide (CN\u207b) to the carbonyl carbon of acetone. The cyanide ion attacks the electrophilic carbonyl carbon, and subsequent protonation of the resulting alkoxide yields the cyanohydrin. This is a classic cyanohydrin reaction \u2014 the ketone form of the Kiliani reaction \u2014 producing a tertiary cyanohydrin due to the symmetrical dimethyl substitution of acetone.\n\n**Step 3**: Acetone cyanohydrin + Hydrogen sulfide \u2192 2-Hydroxy-2-methylpropanethioamide (Thioamide formation)\n- SMILES: CC(C)(O)C#N.S>>CC(C)(O)C(N)=S\n- Reactants: Acetone cyanohydrin (C4H7NO, SMILES: CC(C)(C#N)O) + Hydrogen sulfide (H2S, SMILES: S, InChI: InChI=1S/H2S/h1H2, MW: 34.08)\n- Products: 2-Hydroxy-2-methylpropanethioamide (C4H9NOS, SMILES: CC(C)(C(=S)N)O, InChI: InChI=1S/C4H9NOS/c1-4(2,6)3(5)7/h6H,1-2H3,(H2,5,7), MW: 119.19)\n- Environment: Surface (cyanosulfidic conditions)\n- Mechanism: H2S adds across the nitrile (C\u2261N) triple bond of the cyanohydrin. The sulfur nucleophile attacks the electrophilic nitrile carbon, and proton transfer completes the conversion to a thioamide (C(=S)NH2). This is a key step in the cyanosulfidic framework: conversion of nitriles to thioamides via H2S, which activates the nitrile for subsequent reductive chemistry.\n\n**Step 4**: 2-Hydroxy-2-methylpropanethioamide + HCN + H2S + H2O \u2192 2-Hydroxy-3-methylbutanenitrile + Disulfane + H2S (Reductive cyanide-mediated rearrangement)\n- SMILES: CC(C)(O)C(N)=S.C#N.S.S.O>>CC(C)C(O)C#N.SS.SS.S\n- Reactants: 2-Hydroxy-2-methylpropanethioamide (C4H9NOS) + HCN (CHN) + H2S (H2S) + H2O\n- Products: 2-Hydroxy-3-methylbutanenitrile (C5H9NO, SMILES: CC(C)C(C#N)O, InChI: InChI=1S/C5H9NO/c1-4(2)5(7)3-6/h4-5,7H,1-2H3, MW: 99.13) + Disulfane (H2S2, SMILES: SS, InChI: InChI=1S/H2S2/c1-2/h1-2H, MW: 66.15) + H2S\n- Environment: Surface (cyanosulfidic conditions)\n- Agents: **Sulfidic acid** (H2S, SMILES: S) and **Elemental copper** ([Cu]) as solutes\n- Mechanism: This is a complex, multi-step cyanosulfidic transformation. The thioamide undergoes reductive rearrangement mediated by copper and hydrogen sulfide. The thioamide nitrogen is lost, and a new C\u2013C bond is formed by incorporation of the second equivalent of HCN. Elemental copper acts as a single-electron reductant, while H2S provides reducing equivalents and sulfur acts as a leaving group (forming disulfane, H2S2). The net result is chain extension: the C4 thioamide is converted to a C5 \u03b1-hydroxynitrile with an isopropyl branch, establishing the valine carbon skeleton. This rearrangement is the critical C\u2013C bond-forming step that builds the branched-chain architecture unique to valine.\n\n**Step 5**: 2-Hydroxy-3-methylbutanenitrile + Ammonia \u2192 2-Amino-3-methylbutanenitrile + H2O (Amination)\n- SMILES: CC(C)C(O)C#N.N>>CC(C)C(N)C#N.O\n- Reactants: 2-Hydroxy-3-methylbutanenitrile (C5H9NO) + Ammonia (H3N, SMILES: N, InChI: InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3, MW: 17.03)\n- Products: 2-Amino-3-methylbutanenitrile (C5H10N2, SMILES: CC(C)C(C#N)N, InChI: InChI=1S/C5H10N2/c1-4(2)5(7)3-6/h4-5H,7H2,1-2H3, MW: 98.15) + H2O\n- Environment: Surface (cyanosulfidic conditions)\n- Mechanism: Nucleophilic substitution of the hydroxyl group by ammonia. The \u03b1-hydroxy group is displaced by NH3, converting the hydroxynitrile to an aminonitrile. This is a Strecker-type amination step \u2014 the replacement of OH by NH2 at the \u03b1-carbon adjacent to the nitrile \u2014 producing the direct aminonitrile precursor of valine.\n\n**Step 6**: 2-Amino-3-methylbutanenitrile + 2 H2O \u2192 L-Valine + NH3 (Nitrile hydrolysis)\n- SMILES: CC(C)C(N)C#N.O.O>>CC(C)C(N)C(=O)O.N\n- Reactants: 2-Amino-3-methylbutanenitrile (C5H10N2) + 2 H2O\n- Products: L-Valine (C5H11NO2, SMILES: CC(C)C(C(=O)O)N, InChI: InChI=1S/C5H11NO2/c1-3(2)4(6)5(7)8/h3-4H,6H2,1-2H3,(H,7,8), MW: 117.15) + Ammonia (NH3)\n- Environment: Surface (cyanosulfidic conditions)\n- Mechanism: Hydrolysis of the nitrile group of the aminonitrile to the carboxylic acid. Water attacks the electrophilic nitrile carbon, forming an amide intermediate (2-amino-3-methylbutanamide), which further hydrolyzes to the free carboxylic acid with release of ammonia. This is the final Strecker-type hydrolysis step that converts the aminonitrile to the amino acid L-valine.\n\n### Pathway 2: Cyanosulfidic Protometabolism via Hydroxyacetone (Surface Environment)\n\nA parallel pathway in the same Patel et al. (2015) cyanosulfidic system starts from **hydroxyacetone** instead of dihydroxyacetone. This converges on the same target (L-valine) but proceeds through a C6 aminonitrile intermediate (2-amino-4-methylpentanenitrile), suggesting the cyanosulfidic network produces valine through multiple convergent routes.\n\n**Step 1**: Hydroxyacetone \u2192 Acetone + H2O (Reductive deoxygenation)\n- SMILES: CC(=O)CO.>>CC(C)=O.O\n- Reactants: Hydroxyacetone (C3H6O2, SMILES: CC(=O)CO, InChI: InChI=1S/C3H6O2/c1-3(5)2-4/h4H,2H2,1H3, MW: 74.08)\n- Products: Acetone (C3H6O, SMILES: CC(=O)C) + H2O\n- Environment: Surface (cyanosulfidic conditions)\n- Agents: **Sulfidic acid** (H2S, SMILES: S) and **Elemental copper** ([Cu]) as solutes\n- Mechanism: Reductive deoxygenation of hydroxyacetone (acetol) mediated by copper and hydrogen sulfide under cyanosulfidic conditions. The hydroxymethyl group (\u2013CH2OH) is reduced, removing the oxygen to produce the simpler ketone (acetone). This provides an alternative upstream entry point to the acetone intermediate from a different sugar derivative.\n\n**Step 2**: 2-Amino-4-methylpentanenitrile + 2 H2O \u2192 L-Valine + NH3 (Nitrile hydrolysis)\n- SMILES: CC(C)CC(N)C#N.O.O>>CC(C)C(N)C(=O)O.N\n- Reactants: 2-Amino-4-methylpentanenitrile (C6H12N2, SMILES: CC(C)CC(C#N)N, InChI: InChI=1S/C6H12N2/c1-5(2)3-6(8)4-7/h5-6H,3,8H2,1-2H3, MW: 112.17) + 2 H2O\n- Products: L-Valine (C5H11NO2, SMILES: CC(C)C(C(=O)O)N) + Ammonia (NH3)\n- Environment: Surface (cyanosulfidic conditions)\n- Mechanism: Hydrolysis of the nitrile group of the C6 aminonitrile. Notably, this reaction involves both nitrile hydrolysis and loss of one carbon (decarboxylation or equivalent chain shortening), converting a C6 aminonitrile to the C5 amino acid valine. Water attacks the nitrile carbon, forming the carboxylic acid with release of ammonia. The net loss of one CH2 unit during hydrolysis may occur through a retro-aldol or decarboxylative mechanism under the cyanosulfidic conditions.\n\n## 2. Key Intermediates and Precursors\n\n### Hub Molecule: Acetone (C3H6O)\n- Formula: C3H6O\n- SMILES: CC(=O)C\n- InChI: InChI=1S/C3H6O/c1-3(2)4/h1-2H3\n- MW: 58.08\n- Role: **Central convergence point** \u2014 acetone is produced by two independent upstream reactions (reductive deoxygenation of dihydroxyacetone and of hydroxyacetone) and consumed by the cyanohydrin reaction that initiates the valine carbon skeleton assembly. It is the key branch-point where multiple sugar-derived feedstocks converge before entering the nitrile-based pathway to valine.\n\n### Key Intermediates\n\n**Acetone cyanohydrin** (C4H7NO)\n- Formula: C4H7NO\n- SMILES: CC(C)(C#N)O\n- InChI: InChI=1S/C4H7NO/c1-4(2,6)3-5/h6H,1-2H3\n- MW: 85.10\n- Role: First nitrile intermediate \u2014 formed by HCN addition to acetone, carries the initial C\u2013CN bond that will become the carboxylic acid of valine\n\n**2-Hydroxy-2-methylpropanethioamide** (C4H9NOS)\n- Formula: C4H9NOS\n- SMILES: CC(C)(C(=S)N)O\n- InChI: InChI=1S/C4H9NOS/c1-4(2,6)3(5)7/h6H,1-2H3,(H2,5,7)\n- MW: 119.19\n- Role: Thioamide intermediate \u2014 the H2S-activated form of the cyanohydrin that undergoes copper-mediated reductive rearrangement to build the branched C5 skeleton\n\n**2-Hydroxy-3-methylbutanenitrile** (C5H9NO)\n- Formula: C5H9NO\n- SMILES: CC(C)C(C#N)O\n- InChI: InChI=1S/C5H9NO/c1-4(2)5(7)3-6/h4-5,7H,1-2H3\n- MW: 99.13\n- Role: \u03b1-Hydroxynitrile intermediate \u2014 contains the full valine carbon skeleton (C5, branched) with a hydroxyl group that will be replaced by an amine\n\n**2-Amino-3-methylbutanenitrile** (C5H10N2)\n- Formula: C5H10N2\n- SMILES: CC(C)C(C#N)N\n- InChI: InChI=1S/C5H10N2/c1-4(2)5(7)3-6/h4-5H,7H2,1-2H3\n- MW: 98.15\n- Role: **Direct aminonitrile precursor of valine** \u2014 the Strecker aminonitrile that hydrolyzes to L-valine. This is a key convergence point as it is the last intermediate before the final hydrolysis step.\n\n**2-Amino-4-methylpentanenitrile** (C6H12N2)\n- Formula: C6H12N2\n- SMILES: CC(C)CC(C#N)N\n- InChI: InChI=1S/C6H12N2/c1-5(2)3-6(8)4-7/h5-6H,3,8H2,1-2H3\n- MW: 112.17\n- Role: Alternative aminonitrile \u2014 a C6 aminonitrile from Pathway 2 that also hydrolyzes to valine (with chain shortening)\n\n**Disulfane** (H2S2)\n- Formula: H2S2\n- SMILES: SS\n- InChI: InChI=1S/H2S2/c1-2/h1-2H\n- MW: 66.15\n- Role: Byproduct of the copper/H2S-mediated reductive rearrangement (Step 4 of Pathway 1)\n\n### Starting Molecules\n- **H2O**: SMILES: O, InChI: InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2\n- **HCN** (hydrogen cyanide): SMILES: C#N, InChI: InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H\n- **H2S** (hydrogen sulfide): SMILES: S, InChI: InChI=1S/H2S/h1H2\n- **NH3** (ammonia): SMILES: N, InChI: InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3\n- **Dihydroxyacetone**: SMILES: C(C(=O)CO)O, InChI: InChI=1S/C3H6O3/c4-1-3(6)2-5/h4-5H,1-2H2\n- **Hydroxyacetone**: SMILES: CC(=O)CO, InChI: InChI=1S/C3H6O2/c1-3(5)2-4/h4H,2H2,1H3\n\n## 3. Reaction Mechanisms and Conditions\n\n### Reactions Producing Acetone (Upstream Hub)\n\n**Reaction U1: Dihydroxyacetone reductive deoxygenation (Cu/H2S-mediated)**\n- Dihydroxyacetone \u2192 Acetone + 2 H2O\n- SMILES: O=C(CO)CO>>CC(C)=O.O.O\n- Environment: Surface (cyanosulfidic conditions)\n- Agents: Sulfidic acid (H2S) + Elemental copper ([Cu])\n- Source: Patel, Percivalle, Ritson, Duffy, and Sutherland (2015), *Nature Chemistry*, DOI: 10.1038/nchem.2202\n- Mechanism: Copper/H2S-mediated reductive removal of both hydroxyl groups from the triose sugar dihydroxyacetone, yielding acetone.\n\n**Reaction U2: Hydroxyacetone reductive deoxygenation (Cu/H2S-mediated)**\n- Hydroxyacetone \u2192 Acetone + H2O\n- SMILES: CC(=O)CO.>>CC(C)=O.O\n- Environment: Surface (cyanosulfidic conditions)\n- Agents: Sulfidic acid (H2S) + Elemental copper ([Cu])\n- Source: Patel, Percivalle, Ritson, Duffy, and Sutherland (2015), *Nature Chemistry*, DOI: 10.1038/nchem.2202\n- Mechanism: Copper/H2S-mediated reductive removal of the hydroxyl group from hydroxyacetone (acetol), yielding acetone.\n\n### Reactions Building the Valine Carbon Skeleton (Middle Steps)\n\n**Reaction M1: Cyanohydrin formation**\n- Acetone + HCN \u2192 Acetone cyanohydrin\n- SMILES: CC(C)=O.C#N>>CC(C)(O)C#N\n- Environment: Surface (cyanosulfidic conditions)\n- Source: Patel et al. (2015), *Nature Chemistry*, DOI: 10.1038/nchem.2202\n- Mechanism: Nucleophilic addition of cyanide to the carbonyl of acetone, forming a tertiary cyanohydrin.\n\n**Reaction M2: Thioamide formation**\n- Acetone cyanohydrin + H2S \u2192 2-Hydroxy-2-methylpropanethioamide\n- SMILES: CC(C)(O)C#N.S>>CC(C)(O)C(N)=S\n- Environment: Surface (cyanosulfidic conditions)\n- Source: Patel et al. (2015), *Nature Chemistry*, DOI: 10.1038/nchem.2202\n- Mechanism: H2S addition across the nitrile triple bond, converting the cyanohydrin to a thioamide.\n\n**Reaction M3: Reductive rearrangement (chain extension)**\n- 2-Hydroxy-2-methylpropanethioamide + HCN + H2S + H2O \u2192 2-Hydroxy-3-methylbutanenitrile + Disulfane + H2S\n- SMILES: CC(C)(O)C(N)=S.C#N.S.S.O>>CC(C)C(O)C#N.SS.SS.S\n- Environment: Surface (cyanosulfidic conditions)\n- Agents: Sulfidic acid (H2S) + Elemental copper ([Cu])\n- Source: Patel et al. (2015), *Nature Chemistry*, DOI: 10.1038/nchem.2202\n- Mechanism: Cu/H2S-mediated reductive rearrangement incorporating a second HCN unit. The thioamide is reductively cleaved, and a new C\u2013C bond is formed with HCN, building the branched C5 skeleton. Disulfane (H2S2) is a byproduct of the sulfur-mediated redox chemistry.\n\n**Reaction M4: Amination (Strecker-type)**\n- 2-Hydroxy-3-methylbutanenitrile + NH3 \u2192 2-Amino-3-methylbutanenitrile + H2O\n- SMILES: CC(C)C(O)C#N.N>>CC(C)C(N)C#N.O\n- Environment: Surface (cyanosulfidic conditions)\n- Source: Patel et al. (2015), *Nature Chemistry*, DOI: 10.1038/nchem.2202\n- Mechanism: Nucleophilic substitution of the \u03b1-hydroxyl group by ammonia, converting the hydroxynitrile to the aminonitrile.\n\n### Reactions Producing L-Valine (Final Steps)\n\n**Reaction F1: Nitrile hydrolysis (from C5 aminonitrile)**\n- 2-Amino-3-methylbutanenitrile + 2 H2O \u2192 L-Valine + NH3\n- SMILES: CC(C)C(N)C#N.O.O>>CC(C)C(N)C(=O)O.N\n- Environment: Surface (cyanosulfidic conditions)\n- Source: Patel et al. (2015), *Nature Chemistry*, DOI: 10.1038/nchem.2202\n- Mechanism: Hydrolysis of the nitrile to carboxylic acid via amide intermediate, with release of ammonia.\n\n**Reaction F2: Nitrile hydrolysis (from C6 aminonitrile)**\n- 2-Amino-4-methylpentanenitrile + 2 H2O \u2192 L-Valine + NH3\n- SMILES: CC(C)CC(N)C#N.O.O>>CC(C)C(N)C(=O)O.N\n- Environment: Surface (cyanosulfidic conditions)\n- Source: Patel et al. (2015), *Nature Chemistry*, DOI: 10.1038/nchem.2202\n- Mechanism: Hydrolysis of the C6 aminonitrile nitrile group with concomitant chain shortening to yield the C5 amino acid valine.\n\n## 4. Mineral Catalysts and Surfaces\n\nThe following catalytic agents are experimentally documented for L-valine synthesis reactions in the cyanosulfidic protometabolism framework:\n\n- **Elemental copper (Cu\u2070)**: Metallic copper acts as a single-electron reductant in the cyanosulfidic system. It mediates the reductive deoxygenation of sugar-derived molecules (dihydroxyacetone \u2192 acetone, hydroxyacetone \u2192 acetone) and the reductive rearrangement of thioamides (chain extension step). Copper is oxidized to Cu\u207a or Cu\u00b2\u207a during these transformations. Patel et al. (2015) demonstrated that elemental copper is essential for the reductive steps in the cyanosulfidic network.\n\n- **Sulfidic acid (H2S, hydrogen sulfide)**: H2S plays dual roles \u2014 as a reagent (converting nitriles to thioamides, Step 3) and as a co-reductant with copper in the reductive transformations (Steps 1, 4, 7). In the cyanosulfidic framework, H2S is the sulfur source that gives the chemistry its name. It is oxidized to disulfane (H2S2, SMILES: SS) during reductive steps. H2S is prebiotically available from volcanic outgassing and hydrothermal emissions.\n\nNote: Unlike hydrothermal pathways that use iron-sulfur minerals (greigite, pyrite, magnetite), the valine synthesis pathway operates entirely within the surface cyanosulfidic framework using dissolved copper and H2S as solute agents rather than mineral surfaces.\n\n## 5. Experimental Evidence\n\n### Study 1: Patel et al. (2015)\n- **Title**: \"Common origins of RNA, protein and lipid precursors in a cyanosulfidic protometabolism\"\n- **Journal**: Nature Chemistry\n- **DOI**: 10.1038/nchem.2202\n- **Authors**: Patel B, Percivalle C, Ritson D, Duffy C, Sutherland J\n- **Key finding**: Demonstrated that a unified cyanosulfidic chemistry network starting from hydrogen cyanide (HCN), hydrogen sulfide (H2S), and simple feedstocks (water, ammonia, phosphate) can produce precursors to all three major classes of biomolecules: RNA nucleotides, amino acids (including valine), and lipid precursors. The chemistry operates under surface conditions (UV irradiation, wet-dry cycles in evaporitic pools) with copper and H2S as key redox agents. For valine specifically, the pathway proceeds through: (1) reductive deoxygenation of sugar derivatives (dihydroxyacetone or hydroxyacetone) to acetone using Cu/H2S; (2) cyanohydrin formation with HCN; (3) thioamide conversion with H2S; (4) Cu/H2S-mediated reductive rearrangement with chain extension by a second HCN unit; (5) amination with ammonia; and (6) final nitrile hydrolysis to the amino acid. This establishes that branched-chain amino acids like valine arise naturally from the same cyanosulfidic chemistry that produces simpler amino acids like glycine and alanine, through the incorporation of ketone (rather than aldehyde) substrates.\n\n## 6. Environmental Context\n\n### Surface Environment (Primary \u2014 Cyanosulfidic Pools)\nAll experimentally demonstrated valine synthesis routes operate under surface cyanosulfidic conditions:\n- **Cyanosulfidic protometabolism** (Patel et al., 2015): The entire valine synthesis network operates in surface evaporitic pools where HCN and H2S are available from atmospheric and volcanic sources. UV irradiation drives certain photochemical steps in the broader cyanosulfidic network (though the specific valine reactions may not require UV directly). Wet-dry cycling in geothermal surface pools concentrates reactants and drives dehydration steps.\n- **Temperature**: Ambient to mildly elevated (300-370 K), characteristic of surface geothermal environments.\n- **Key reagents**: HCN (from atmospheric chemistry, volcanic outgassing), H2S (from volcanic emissions), Cu (from mineral dissolution), NH3 (from atmospheric/volcanic sources), sugar derivatives (dihydroxyacetone, hydroxyacetone \u2014 from formose-type chemistry or photochemical synthesis).\n\n### Hydrothermal Connection\nWhile the valine pathway itself operates under surface conditions, the upstream sugar feedstocks (dihydroxyacetone, hydroxyacetone) could originate from formose-type chemistry in hydrothermal settings or from serpentinization-driven synthesis of formaldehyde (H2CO), which undergoes aldol condensation to form C3 sugars. This provides a potential hydrothermal \u2192 surface flow of carbon feedstocks.\n\n### Environmental Interconnection\n1. **Hydrothermal \u2192 Surface**: Simple C1 molecules (H2CO, HCN) and C3 sugar derivatives produced at hydrothermal vents or by volcanic outgassing are transported to surface pools where cyanosulfidic chemistry operates.\n2. **Surface \u2192 Biochemical**: L-valine produced in surface cyanosulfidic pools enters the prebiotic amino acid pool available for peptide bond formation and proto-metabolic assembly. Valine is one of the 20 canonical amino acids and a key hydrophobic residue in proteins.\n\n## 7. Open Questions and Challenges\n\n1. **Chirality**: The cyanosulfidic pathway produces racemic valine (equal L and D). The origin of homochirality (biological preference for L-amino acids) remains unresolved for valine. Enantiomeric enrichment mechanisms such as asymmetric adsorption on chiral mineral surfaces, circularly polarized light, or amplification through crystal-liquid equilibria have been proposed but not demonstrated specifically for valine.\n\n2. **Acetone availability**: The pathway requires acetone, which is produced by reductive deoxygenation of sugar derivatives (dihydroxyacetone, hydroxyacetone). The prebiotic availability of these C3 sugars depends on the formose reaction or alternative sugar synthesis routes, whose efficiency and selectivity under prebiotic conditions are debated.\n\n3. **Copper availability**: Elemental copper (Cu\u2070) is essential for the reductive steps. While copper minerals exist in early Earth geological settings, the concentration and bioavailability of dissolved Cu\u2070 in prebiotic surface pools needs quantitative constraints. Copper could be released from sulfide minerals (e.g., chalcopyrite) under the acidic or reducing conditions of cyanosulfidic environments.\n\n4. **Selectivity**: The cyanosulfidic network produces many products simultaneously (amino acids, nucleotide precursors, lipid precursors). The selectivity and yield for valine specifically, versus competing products like leucine (from the C6 aminonitrile pathway) or other branched-chain amino acids, needs further quantification.\n\n5. **Chain extension mechanism**: The reductive rearrangement step (Step 4 of Pathway 1) involving Cu/H2S-mediated chain extension through thioamide intermediates is mechanistically complex and not fully characterized at the molecular level. The detailed radical or polar mechanism of the C\u2013C bond formation during this step remains an active area of study.\n\n6. **Pathway 2 chain shortening**: The conversion of the C6 aminonitrile (2-amino-4-methylpentanenitrile) to C5 valine involves apparent loss of one carbon during hydrolysis. The mechanism of this chain shortening \u2014 whether through retro-aldol, decarboxylation, or another process \u2014 needs clarification.\n\n=== END CURATED LITERATURE REVIEW ===\n\n=== E