{"timestamp": "2026-10-19T17:25:30.330299", "agent_name": "SynthesisNetworkAgent", "method_name": "generate_synthesis_network", "step": null, "attempt": null, "system_message": "\n# SYNTHESIS NETWORK AGENT - Complete Prebiotic Synthesis Network Generation\n\nYou are an expert prebiotic geochemist who designs complete synthesis networks from simple\nmolecules to complex biomolecules. You synthesize knowledge across three terrestrial prebiotic\nenvironments:\n\n1. **Hydrothermal Environments** (350-600K, high pressure, deep-sea vents)\n2. **Surface Environments** (300-370K, ambient pressure, wet-dry cycles, UV exposure)\n3. **Biochemical/Prebiotic** (variable conditions, complex biomolecule assembly)\n\nYour task is to generate a COMPLETE SYNTHESIS NETWORK in a SINGLE response - not individual steps.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 1: HYDROTHERMAL ENVIRONMENTS\n\n## Context\nDeep-sea hydrothermal systems. High pressure, temperatures between 350-600K, pH gradients\nacross vent walls, and mineral surfaces.\n\n## Environments\n- **Alkaline Hydrothermal Vents**: 350-450K, pH 9-11, H\u2082-rich, serpentinization-driven\n- **Black Smoker Vents**: 500-650K, acidic, metal-sulfide rich\n- **Subseafloor Hydrothermal**: 370-470K, moderate pH, rock-water interactions\n\n## Minerals Present in This Setting (examples, non-exhaustive)\nIron sulfides (e.g., mackinawite, greigite, pyrite), iron oxides (e.g., magnetite), native metals\nand alloys. Use a mineral only if it is chemically justified for the specific reaction.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 2: SURFACE ENVIRONMENTS\n\n## Context\nAmbient pressure, temperatures between 300-370K, wet-dry cycles, UV exposure, and evaporitic\npools.\n\n## Environments\n- **Evaporitic Pools**: 300-370K, wet-dry cycles, mineral-rich shores\n- **Volcanic Hot Springs**: 320-370K, acidic, sulfur-rich, boron minerals\n- **Tidal Flats**: 280-330K, periodic wetting/drying, UV exposure\n- **Subaerial Geothermal Fields**: 300-370K, steam vents, mineral surfaces\n\n## Minerals Present in This Setting (examples, non-exhaustive)\nClays (e.g., montmorillonite), phosphate minerals, borate minerals, metal sulfides, metal oxides.\nUse a mineral only if it is chemically justified for the specific reaction.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 3: BIOCHEMICAL/PREBIOTIC\n\n## Context\nAssembly of more complex biomolecules under plausible prebiotic scenarios, under variable\nconditions including pools, interfaces, and mineral surfaces.\n\n## Environments\n- **Prebiotic Pools**: Concentrated solutions of organic molecules\n- **Mineral Interfaces**: Mineral surfaces that can adsorb or template reactants\n- **Lipid Membranes**: Protocell-like compartments for concentrated chemistry\n- **Geothermal Cycling**: Repeated heating/cooling\n\n## Global Constraints (all environments)\n- NO molecular oxygen (anoxic early Earth)\n- Strong UV radiation at the surface (no ozone layer)\n- Iron abundant in reduced form (Fe\u00b2\u207a)\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 4: NETWORK STRUCTURE REQUIREMENTS\n\nYour output must be a BIPARTITE GRAPH with two distinct node types:\n\n## 1. MOLECULE NODES\n- Represent chemical species (substrates, products, intermediates)\n- Each molecule appears ONCE in the network, even if used by multiple reactions\n- Include: id, formula, IUPAC name, common name, InChI, role, environment_formed\n\n## 2. REACTION NODES\n- Each reaction is a separate node with a unique ID (e.g., \"rxn_001\")\n- Has INPUT molecules (reactants) and OUTPUT molecules (products)\n- Includes: id, name, inputs[], outputs[], environment, agents[], conditions, mechanism, reasoning\n- List each molecule ID at most once in inputs[] and once in outputs[]; stoichiometric coefficients\n  are not represented and are inferred automatically, so never repeat an ID to express them\n\n## NEUTRALITY (CRITICAL)\n- No starting feedstock, intermediate, reaction family, or prebiotic framework is preferred.\n  Choose routes solely on chemical and geochemical merit for this specific target.\n- Environments may be used in any order and in any combination; a network that uses only one\n  environment is acceptable if that is what the chemistry supports.\n- Do not add intermediates, shared nodes, or cross-environment transfers merely to make the\n  network look interconnected. Include them only when the chemistry requires them.\n\n## DESIGN PRINCIPLES\n1. **Agents/catalysts**: Specify a catalyst or mineral agent in the \"agents\" field when one is\n   used and justify it; use null when no catalyst is needed.\n2. **Reaction diversity**: Use whatever reaction classes the chemistry of the target requires.\n3. **Alternative routes**: Where the chemistry supports more than one route, include them as\n   separate pathways.\n\n## NETWORK STRUCTURE REQUIREMENTS\n\n- Minimum 8-15 molecule nodes\n- Minimum 6-12 reaction nodes\n- No isolated subgraphs - all nodes must be connected\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 5: REQUIRED JSON OUTPUT FORMAT\n\nYou MUST output a JSON object with this exact structure:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"<name of target>\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"<high-level explanation of the synthesis approach>\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"<chemical formula>\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"<IUPAC name>\",\n      \"common_name\": \"<common name or null>\",\n      \"inchi\": \"<InChI string or null>\",\n      \"role\": \"<starting | intermediate | hub_intermediate | target>\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"<Hydrothermal | Surface | Biochemical | null>\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"<descriptive reaction name>\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"<Hydrothermal | Surface | Biochemical>\",\n      \"agents\": [\"<catalyst/mineral agent, if any>\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"<e.g., 350-400K>\",\n        \"pressure\": \"<e.g., high, 1 atm>\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"<catalyst name, or null>\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"<true | false>\",\n        \"other\": \"<additional conditions>\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"<brief mechanism description>\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"<scientific justification>\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"<descriptive pathway name>\",\n      \"description\": \"<pathway description>\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_007\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_008\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\"<mol_id, if any>\"],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_005\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_009\"]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nNotes on \"hub_molecules\" and \"convergence_points\": these are descriptive fields only. List any\nmolecules that, in the network you designed, happen to be consumed or produced by multiple reactions.\nThey may be empty lists. Do not design the network to fill them.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 6: LITERATURE-GUIDED SYNTHESIS (when research findings are provided)\n\nWhen research findings are provided, use them as evidence to inform your design:\n\n1. **Weigh the evidence**:\n   - Prefer routes and conditions that are well supported experimentally in the provided research\n   - Take into account the critiques, contested points, and limitations the research reports\n   - You may depart from the routes described in the research when the chemistry justifies it;\n     state the justification explicitly in the \"reasoning\" field\n\n2. **CITE papers** in the \"reasoning\" field of each reaction:\n   - Reference specific studies by author and year, e.g., \"(Author, Year)\"\n   - When multiple studies support a reaction, cite the most relevant ones\n   - If a reaction has no literature support from the provided research, explicitly state it is speculative and justify why it is necessary\n\n3. **ADDRESS gaps**: If the research identifies open questions or challenges, acknowledge them in your reasoning\n\n---\n\n# IMPORTANT GUIDELINES\n\n1. Generate the ENTIRE network in one response - do not propose individual steps\n2. Ensure molecules are defined BEFORE being referenced in reactions\n3. Every molecule ID in reactions must exist in the molecules array\n4. Pathways must trace valid routes through the reaction network\n5. Consider realistic prebiotic conditions for each reaction's stated environment\n6. Include rate-limiting or key bottleneck reactions\n7. The network should tell a coherent chemical story from simple to complex\n8. When deep research findings are provided, cite relevant literature in reaction reasoning fields\n", "user_message": "\nGenerate a SYNTHESIS NETWORK for: Valine\n\nREQUIREMENTS:\n- Generate a bipartite network with molecule nodes and reaction nodes\n- Include 10 distinct pathways to the target\n- Ensure at least 8-15 molecules and 6-12 reactions\n- Choose feedstocks, intermediates, reaction types, and environments on chemical merit alone\n\nOutput the complete network as JSON following the schema in your instructions.\n\n\n=== LITERATURE RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\nThe following deep research report contains peer-reviewed literature on prebiotic synthesis\nof Valine. Use it as evidence to inform your network design.\n\n=== CURATED LITERATURE REVIEW ===\n# Prebiotic Synthesis of L-Valine: Comprehensive Literature Review\n\n## 1. Known Prebiotic Synthesis Routes\n\nTwo experimentally demonstrated pathways produce L-valine (C5H11NO2, SMILES: CC(C)C(C(=O)O)N, InChI: InChI=1S/C5H11NO2/c1-3(2)4(6)5(7)8/h3-4H,6H2,1-2H3,(H,7,8)) from prebiotically available precursors. Both operate within the cyanosulfidic protometabolism framework under surface environment conditions.\n\n### Pathway 1: Cyanosulfidic Protometabolism via Dihydroxyacetone (Surface Environment)\n\nThe cyanosulfidic protometabolism pathway demonstrated by Patel, Percivalle, Ritson, Duffy, and Sutherland (2015, *Nature Chemistry*, DOI: 10.1038/nchem.2202) constructs L-valine through a 6-step sequence starting from dihydroxyacetone, proceeding through acetone and a series of nitrile intermediates. This pathway follows the general cyanosulfidic strategy: sugar-derived ketones undergo cyanohydrin formation, thioamide conversion, reductive rearrangement with copper/H2S, amination, and final nitrile hydrolysis to the amino acid.\n\n**Step 1**: Dihydroxyacetone \u2192 Acetone + 2 H2O (Reductive deoxygenation)\n- SMILES: O=C(CO)CO>>CC(C)=O.O.O\n- Reactants: Dihydroxyacetone (C3H6O3, SMILES: C(C(=O)CO)O, InChI: InChI=1S/C3H6O3/c4-1-3(6)2-5/h4-5H,1-2H2, MW: 90.08)\n- Products: Acetone (C3H6O, SMILES: CC(=O)C, InChI: InChI=1S/C3H6O/c1-3(2)4/h1-2H3, MW: 58.08) + H2O\n- Environment: Surface (cyanosulfidic conditions)\n- Agents: **Sulfidic acid** (H2S, SMILES: S) and **Elemental copper** ([Cu]) as solutes\n- Mechanism: Reductive deoxygenation of the triose sugar dihydroxyacetone (DHA) under cyanosulfidic conditions. Copper acts as a single-electron reductant in concert with hydrogen sulfide, stripping the hydroxymethyl groups and reducing the C3 backbone to acetone. This converts a sugar feedstock into a simple ketone suitable for downstream cyanohydrin chemistry. The reaction is analogous to the broader Patel et al. demonstration that cyanosulfidic chemistry can interconvert sugars and metabolic intermediates.\n\n**Step 2**: Acetone + Hydrogen cyanide \u2192 Acetone cyanohydrin (Cyanohydrin formation)\n- SMILES: CC(C)=O.C#N>>CC(C)(O)C#N\n- Reactants: Acetone (C3H6O, SMILES: CC(=O)C) + Hydrogen cyanide (CHN, SMILES: C#N, InChI: InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H, MW: 27.03)\n- Products: Acetone cyanohydrin (C4H7NO, SMILES: CC(C)(C#N)O, InChI: InChI=1S/C4H7NO/c1-4(2,6)3-5/h6H,1-2H3, MW: 85.10)\n- Environment: Surface (cyanosulfidic conditions)\n- Mechanism: Nucleophilic addition of cyanide (CN\u207b) to the carbonyl carbon of acetone. The cyanide ion attacks the electrophilic carbonyl carbon, and subsequent protonation of the resulting alkoxide yields the cyanohydrin. This is a classic cyanohydrin reaction \u2014 the ketone form of the Kiliani reaction \u2014 producing a tertiary cyanohydrin due to the symmetrical dimethyl substitution of acetone.\n\n**Step 3**: Acetone cyanohydrin + Hydrogen sulfide \u2192 2-Hydroxy-2-methylpropanethioamide (Thioamide formation)\n- SMILES: CC(C)(O)C#N.S>>CC(C)(O)C(N)=S\n- Reactants: Acetone cyanohydrin (C4H7NO, SMILES: CC(C)(C#N)O) + Hydrogen sulfide (H2S, SMILES: S, InChI: InChI=1S/H2S/h1H2, MW: 34.08)\n- Products: 2-Hydroxy-2-methylpropanethioamide (C4H9NOS, SMILES: CC(C)(C(=S)N)O, InChI: InChI=1S/C4H9NOS/c1-4(2,6)3(5)7/h6H,1-2H3,(H2,5,7), MW: 119.19)\n- Environment: Surface (cyanosulfidic conditions)\n- Mechanism: H2S adds across the nitrile (C\u2261N) triple bond of the cyanohydrin. The sulfur nucleophile attacks the electrophilic nitrile carbon, and proton transfer completes the conversion to a thioamide (C(=S)NH2). This is a key step in the cyanosulfidic framework: conversion of nitriles to thioamides via H2S, which activates the nitrile for subsequent reductive chemistry.\n\n**Step 4**: 2-Hydroxy-2-methylpropanethioamide + HCN + H2S + H2O \u2192 2-Hydroxy-3-methylbutanenitrile + Disulfane + H2S (Reductive cyanide-mediated rearrangement)\n- SMILES: CC(C)(O)C(N)=S.C#N.S.S.O>>CC(C)C(O)C#N.SS.SS.S\n- Reactants: 2-Hydroxy-2-methylpropanethioamide (C4H9NOS) + HCN (CHN) + H2S (H2S) + H2O\n- Products: 2-Hydroxy-3-methylbutanenitrile (C5H9NO, SMILES: CC(C)C(C#N)O, InChI: InChI=1S/C5H9NO/c1-4(2)5(7)3-6/h4-5,7H,1-2H3, MW: 99.13) + Disulfane (H2S2, SMILES: SS, InChI: InChI=1S/H2S2/c1-2/h1-2H, MW: 66.15) + H2S\n- Environment: Surface (cyanosulfidic conditions)\n- Agents: **Sulfidic acid** (H2S, SMILES: S) and **Elemental copper** ([Cu]) as solutes\n- Mechanism: This is a complex, multi-step cyanosulfidic transformation. The thioamide undergoes reductive rearrangement mediated by copper and hydrogen sulfide. The thioamide nitrogen is lost, and a new C\u2013C bond is formed by incorporation of the second equivalent of HCN. Elemental copper acts as a single-electron reductant, while H2S provides reducing equivalents and sulfur acts as a leaving group (forming disulfane, H2S2). The net result is chain extension: the C4 thioamide is converted to a C5 \u03b1-hydroxynitrile with an isopropyl branch, establishing the valine carbon skeleton. This rearrangement is the critical C\u2013C bond-forming step that builds the branched-chain architecture unique to valine.\n\n**Step 5**: 2-Hydroxy-3-methylbutanenitrile + Ammonia \u2192 2-Amino-3-methylbutanenitrile + H2O (Amination)\n- SMILES: CC(C)C(O)C#N.N>>CC(C)C(N)C#N.O\n- Reactants: 2-Hydroxy-3-methylbutanenitrile (C5H9NO) + Ammonia (H3N, SMILES: N, InChI: InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3, MW: 17.03)\n- Products: 2-Amino-3-methylbutanenitrile (C5H10N2, SMILES: CC(C)C(C#N)N, InChI: InChI=1S/C5H10N2/c1-4(2)5(7)3-6/h4-5H,7H2,1-2H3, MW: 98.15) + H2O\n- Environment: Surface (cyanosulfidic conditions)\n- Mechanism: Nucleophilic substitution of the hydroxyl group by ammonia. The \u03b1-hydroxy group is displaced by NH3, converting the hydroxynitrile to an aminonitrile. This is a Strecker-type amination step \u2014 the replacement of OH by NH2 at the \u03b1-carbon adjacent to the nitrile \u2014 producing the direct aminonitrile precursor of valine.\n\n**Step 6**: 2-Amino-3-methylbutanenitrile + 2 H2O \u2192 L-Valine + NH3 (Nitrile hydrolysis)\n- SMILES: CC(C)C(N)C#N.O.O>>CC(C)C(N)C(=O)O.N\n- Reactants: 2-Amino-3-methylbutanenitrile (C5H10N2) + 2 H2O\n- Products: L-Valine (C5H11NO2, SMILES: CC(C)C(C(=O)O)N, InChI: InChI=1S/C5H11NO2/c1-3(2)4(6)5(7)8/h3-4H,6H2,1-2H3,(H,7,8), MW: 117.15) + Ammonia (NH3)\n- Environment: Surface (cyanosulfidic conditions)\n- Mechanism: Hydrolysis of the nitrile group of the aminonitrile to the carboxylic acid. Water attacks the electrophilic nitrile carbon, forming an amide intermediate (2-amino-3-methylbutanamide), which further hydrolyzes to the free carboxylic acid with release of ammonia. This is the final Strecker-type hydrolysis step that converts the aminonitrile to the amino acid L-valine.\n\n### Pathway 2: Cyanosulfidic Protometabolism via Hydroxyacetone (Surface Environment)\n\nA parallel pathway in the same Patel et al. (2015) cyanosulfidic system starts from **hydroxyacetone** instead of dihydroxyacetone. This converges on the same target (L-valine) but proceeds through a C6 aminonitrile intermediate (2-amino-4-methylpentanenitrile), suggesting the cyanosulfidic network produces valine through multiple convergent routes.\n\n**Step 1**: Hydroxyacetone \u2192 Acetone + H2O (Reductive deoxygenation)\n- SMILES: CC(=O)CO.>>CC(C)=O.O\n- Reactants: Hydroxyacetone (C3H6O2, SMILES: CC(=O)CO, InChI: InChI=1S/C3H6O2/c1-3(5)2-4/h4H,2H2,1H3, MW: 74.08)\n- Products: Acetone (C3H6O, SMILES: CC(=O)C) + H2O\n- Environment: Surface (cyanosulfidic conditions)\n- Agents: **Sulfidic acid** (H2S, SMILES: S) and **Elemental copper** ([Cu]) as solutes\n- Mechanism: Reductive deoxygenation of hydroxyacetone (acetol) mediated by copper and hydrogen sulfide under cyanosulfidic conditions. The hydroxymethyl group (\u2013CH2OH) is reduced, removing the oxygen to produce the simpler ketone (acetone). This provides an alternative upstream entry point to the acetone intermediate from a different sugar derivative.\n\n**Step 2**: 2-Amino-4-methylpentanenitrile + 2 H2O \u2192 L-Valine + NH3 (Nitrile hydrolysis)\n- SMILES: CC(C)CC(N)C#N.O.O>>CC(C)C(N)C(=O)O.N\n- Reactants: 2-Amino-4-methylpentanenitrile (C6H12N2, SMILES: CC(C)CC(C#N)N, InChI: InChI=1S/C6H12N2/c1-5(2)3-6(8)4-7/h5-6H,3,8H2,1-2H3, MW: 112.17) + 2 H2O\n- Products: L-Valine (C5H11NO2, SMILES: CC(C)C(C(=O)O)N) + Ammonia (NH3)\n- Environment: Surface (cyanosulfidic conditions)\n- Mechanism: Hydrolysis of the nitrile group of the C6 aminonitrile. Notably, this reaction involves both nitrile hydrolysis and loss of one carbon (decarboxylation or equivalent chain shortening), converting a C6 aminonitrile to the C5 amino acid valine. Water attacks the nitrile carbon, forming the carboxylic acid with release of ammonia. The net loss of one CH2 unit during hydrolysis may occur through a retro-aldol or decarboxylative mechanism under the cyanosulfidic conditions.\n\n## 2. Key Intermediates and Precursors\n\n### Hub Molecule: Acetone (C3H6O)\n- Formula: C3H6O\n- SMILES: CC(=O)C\n- InChI: InChI=1S/C3H6O/c1-3(2)4/h1-2H3\n- MW: 58.08\n- Role: **Central convergence point** \u2014 acetone is produced by two independent upstream reactions (reductive deoxygenation of dihydroxyacetone and of hydroxyacetone) and consumed by the cyanohydrin reaction that initiates the valine carbon skeleton assembly. It is the key branch-point where multiple sugar-derived feedstocks converge before entering the nitrile-based pathway to valine.\n\n### Key Intermediates\n\n**Acetone cyanohydrin** (C4H7NO)\n- Formula: C4H7NO\n- SMILES: CC(C)(C#N)O\n- InChI: InChI=1S/C4H7NO/c1-4(2,6)3-5/h6H,1-2H3\n- MW: 85.10\n- Role: First nitrile intermediate \u2014 formed by HCN addition to acetone, carries the initial C\u2013CN bond that will become the carboxylic acid of valine\n\n**2-Hydroxy-2-methylpropanethioamide** (C4H9NOS)\n- Formula: C4H9NOS\n- SMILES: CC(C)(C(=S)N)O\n- InChI: InChI=1S/C4H9NOS/c1-4(2,6)3(5)7/h6H,1-2H3,(H2,5,7)\n- MW: 119.19\n- Role: Thioamide intermediate \u2014 the H2S-activated form of the cyanohydrin that undergoes copper-mediated reductive rearrangement to build the branched C5 skeleton\n\n**2-Hydroxy-3-methylbutanenitrile** (C5H9NO)\n- Formula: C5H9NO\n- SMILES: CC(C)C(C#N)O\n- InChI: InChI=1S/C5H9NO/c1-4(2)5(7)3-6/h4-5,7H,1-2H3\n- MW: 99.13\n- Role: \u03b1-Hydroxynitrile intermediate \u2014 contains the full valine carbon skeleton (C5, branched) with a hydroxyl group that will be replaced by an amine\n\n**2-Amino-3-methylbutanenitrile** (C5H10N2)\n- Formula: C5H10N2\n- SMILES: CC(C)C(C#N)N\n- InChI: InChI=1S/C5H10N2/c1-4(2)5(7)3-6/h4-5H,7H2,1-2H3\n- MW: 98.15\n- Role: **Direct aminonitrile precursor of valine** \u2014 the Strecker aminonitrile that hydrolyzes to L-valine. This is a key convergence point as it is the last intermediate before the final hydrolysis step.\n\n**2-Amino-4-methylpentanenitrile** (C6H12N2)\n- Formula: C6H12N2\n- SMILES: CC(C)CC(C#N)N\n- InChI: InChI=1S/C6H12N2/c1-5(2)3-6(8)4-7/h5-6H,3,8H2,1-2H3\n- MW: 112.17\n- Role: Alternative aminonitrile \u2014 a C6 aminonitrile from Pathway 2 that also hydrolyzes to valine (with chain shortening)\n\n**Disulfane** (H2S2)\n- Formula: H2S2\n- SMILES: SS\n- InChI: InChI=1S/H2S2/c1-2/h1-2H\n- MW: 66.15\n- Role: Byproduct of the copper/H2S-mediated reductive rearrangement (Step 4 of Pathway 1)\n\n### Starting Molecules\n- **H2O**: SMILES: O, InChI: InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2\n- **HCN** (hydrogen cyanide): SMILES: C#N, InChI: InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H\n- **H2S** (hydrogen sulfide): SMILES: S, InChI: InChI=1S/H2S/h1H2\n- **NH3** (ammonia): SMILES: N, InChI: InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3\n- **Dihydroxyacetone**: SMILES: C(C(=O)CO)O, InChI: InChI=1S/C3H6O3/c4-1-3(6)2-5/h4-5H,1-2H2\n- **Hydroxyacetone**: SMILES: CC(=O)CO, InChI: InChI=1S/C3H6O2/c1-3(5)2-4/h4H,2H2,1H3\n\n## 3. Reaction Mechanisms and Conditions\n\n### Reactions Producing Acetone (Upstream Hub)\n\n**Reaction U1: Dihydroxyacetone reductive deoxygenation (Cu/H2S-mediated)**\n- Dihydroxyacetone \u2192 Acetone + 2 H2O\n- SMILES: O=C(CO)CO>>CC(C)=O.O.O\n- Environment: Surface (cyanosulfidic conditions)\n- Agents: Sulfidic acid (H2S) + Elemental copper ([Cu])\n- Source: Patel, Percivalle, Ritson, Duffy, and Sutherland (2015), *Nature Chemistry*, DOI: 10.1038/nchem.2202\n- Mechanism: Copper/H2S-mediated reductive removal of both hydroxyl groups from the triose sugar dihydroxyacetone, yielding acetone.\n\n**Reaction U2: Hydroxyacetone reductive deoxygenation (Cu/H2S-mediated)**\n- Hydroxyacetone \u2192 Acetone + H2O\n- SMILES: CC(=O)CO.>>CC(C)=O.O\n- Environment: Surface (cyanosulfidic conditions)\n- Agents: Sulfidic acid (H2S) + Elemental copper ([Cu])\n- Source: Patel, Percivalle, Ritson, Duffy, and Sutherland (2015), *Nature Chemistry*, DOI: 10.1038/nchem.2202\n- Mechanism: Copper/H2S-mediated reductive removal of the hydroxyl group from hydroxyacetone (acetol), yielding acetone.\n\n### Reactions Building the Valine Carbon Skeleton (Middle Steps)\n\n**Reaction M1: Cyanohydrin formation**\n- Acetone + HCN \u2192 Acetone cyanohydrin\n- SMILES: CC(C)=O.C#N>>CC(C)(O)C#N\n- Environment: Surface (cyanosulfidic conditions)\n- Source: Patel et al. (2015), *Nature Chemistry*, DOI: 10.1038/nchem.2202\n- Mechanism: Nucleophilic addition of cyanide to the carbonyl of acetone, forming a tertiary cyanohydrin.\n\n**Reaction M2: Thioamide formation**\n- Acetone cyanohydrin + H2S \u2192 2-Hydroxy-2-methylpropanethioamide\n- SMILES: CC(C)(O)C#N.S>>CC(C)(O)C(N)=S\n- Environment: Surface (cyanosulfidic conditions)\n- Source: Patel et al. (2015), *Nature Chemistry*, DOI: 10.1038/nchem.2202\n- Mechanism: H2S addition across the nitrile triple bond, converting the cyanohydrin to a thioamide.\n\n**Reaction M3: Reductive rearrangement (chain extension)**\n- 2-Hydroxy-2-methylpropanethioamide + HCN + H2S + H2O \u2192 2-Hydroxy-3-methylbutanenitrile + Disulfane + H2S\n- SMILES: CC(C)(O)C(N)=S.C#N.S.S.O>>CC(C)C(O)C#N.SS.SS.S\n- Environment: Surface (cyanosulfidic conditions)\n- Agents: Sulfidic acid (H2S) + Elemental copper ([Cu])\n- Source: Patel et al. (2015), *Nature Chemistry*, DOI: 10.1038/nchem.2202\n- Mechanism: Cu/H2S-mediated reductive rearrangement incorporating a second HCN unit. The thioamide is reductively cleaved, and a new C\u2013C bond is formed with HCN, building the branched C5 skeleton. Disulfane (H2S2) is a byproduct of the sulfur-mediated redox chemistry.\n\n**Reaction M4: Amination (Strecker-type)**\n- 2-Hydroxy-3-methylbutanenitrile + NH3 \u2192 2-Amino-3-methylbutanenitrile + H2O\n- SMILES: CC(C)C(O)C#N.N>>CC(C)C(N)C#N.O\n- Environment: Surface (cyanosulfidic conditions)\n- Source: Patel et al. (2015), *Nature Chemistry*, DOI: 10.1038/nchem.2202\n- Mechanism: Nucleophilic substitution of the \u03b1-hydroxyl group by ammonia, converting the hydroxynitrile to the aminonitrile.\n\n### Reactions Producing L-Valine (Final Steps)\n\n**Reaction F1: Nitrile hydrolysis (from C5 aminonitrile)**\n- 2-Amino-3-methylbutanenitrile + 2 H2O \u2192 L-Valine + NH3\n- SMILES: CC(C)C(N)C#N.O.O>>CC(C)C(N)C(=O)O.N\n- Environment: Surface (cyanosulfidic conditions)\n- Source: Patel et al. (2015), *Nature Chemistry*, DOI: 10.1038/nchem.2202\n- Mechanism: Hydrolysis of the nitrile to carboxylic acid via amide intermediate, with release of ammonia.\n\n**Reaction F2: Nitrile hydrolysis (from C6 aminonitrile)**\n- 2-Amino-4-methylpentanenitrile + 2 H2O \u2192 L-Valine + NH3\n- SMILES: CC(C)CC(N)C#N.O.O>>CC(C)C(N)C(=O)O.N\n- Environment: Surface (cyanosulfidic conditions)\n- Source: Patel et al. (2015), *Nature Chemistry*, DOI: 10.1038/nchem.2202\n- Mechanism: Hydrolysis of the C6 aminonitrile nitrile group with concomitant chain shortening to yield the C5 amino acid valine.\n\n## 4. Mineral Catalysts and Surfaces\n\nThe following catalytic agents are experimentally documented for L-valine synthesis reactions in the cyanosulfidic protometabolism framework:\n\n- **Elemental copper (Cu\u2070)**: Metallic copper acts as a single-electron reductant in the cyanosulfidic system. It mediates the reductive deoxygenation of sugar-derived molecules (dihydroxyacetone \u2192 acetone, hydroxyacetone \u2192 acetone) and the reductive rearrangement of thioamides (chain extension step). Copper is oxidized to Cu\u207a or Cu\u00b2\u207a during these transformations. Patel et al. (2015) demonstrated that elemental copper is essential for the reductive steps in the cyanosulfidic network.\n\n- **Sulfidic acid (H2S, hydrogen sulfide)**: H2S plays dual roles \u2014 as a reagent (converting nitriles to thioamides, Step 3) and as a co-reductant with copper in the reductive transformations (Steps 1, 4, 7). In the cyanosulfidic framework, H2S is the sulfur source that gives the chemistry its name. It is oxidized to disulfane (H2S2, SMILES: SS) during reductive steps. H2S is prebiotically available from volcanic outgassing and hydrothermal emissions.\n\nNote: Unlike hydrothermal pathways that use iron-sulfur minerals (greigite, pyrite, magnetite), the valine synthesis pathway operates entirely within the surface cyanosulfidic framework using dissolved copper and H2S as solute agents rather than mineral surfaces.\n\n## 5. Experimental Evidence\n\n### Study 1: Patel et al. (2015)\n- **Title**: \"Common origins of RNA, protein and lipid precursors in a cyanosulfidic protometabolism\"\n- **Journal**: Nature Chemistry\n- **DOI**: 10.1038/nchem.2202\n- **Authors**: Patel B, Percivalle C, Ritson D, Duffy C, Sutherland J\n- **Key finding**: Demonstrated that a unified cyanosulfidic chemistry network starting from hydrogen cyanide (HCN), hydrogen sulfide (H2S), and simple feedstocks (water, ammonia, phosphate) can produce precursors to all three major classes of biomolecules: RNA nucleotides, amino acids (including valine), and lipid precursors. The chemistry operates under surface conditions (UV irradiation, wet-dry cycles in evaporitic pools) with copper and H2S as key redox agents. For valine specifically, the pathway proceeds through: (1) reductive deoxygenation of sugar derivatives (dihydroxyacetone or hydroxyacetone) to acetone using Cu/H2S; (2) cyanohydrin formation with HCN; (3) thioamide conversion with H2S; (4) Cu/H2S-mediated reductive rearrangement with chain extension by a second HCN unit; (5) amination with ammonia; and (6) final nitrile hydrolysis to the amino acid. This establishes that branched-chain amino acids like valine arise naturally from the same cyanosulfidic chemistry that produces simpler amino acids like glycine and alanine, through the incorporation of ketone (rather than aldehyde) substrates.\n\n## 6. Environmental Context\n\n### Surface Environment (Primary \u2014 Cyanosulfidic Pools)\nAll experimentally demonstrated valine synthesis routes operate under surface cyanosulfidic conditions:\n- **Cyanosulfidic protometabolism** (Patel et al., 2015): The entire valine synthesis network operates in surface evaporitic pools where HCN and H2S are available from atmospheric and volcanic sources. UV irradiation drives certain photochemical steps in the broader cyanosulfidic network (though the specific valine reactions may not require UV directly). Wet-dry cycling in geothermal surface pools concentrates reactants and drives dehydration steps.\n- **Temperature**: Ambient to mildly elevated (300-370 K), characteristic of surface geothermal environments.\n- **Key reagents**: HCN (from atmospheric chemistry, volcanic outgassing), H2S (from volcanic emissions), Cu (from mineral dissolution), NH3 (from atmospheric/volcanic sources), sugar derivatives (dihydroxyacetone, hydroxyacetone \u2014 from formose-type chemistry or photochemical synthesis).\n\n### Hydrothermal Connection\nWhile the valine pathway itself operates under surface conditions, the upstream sugar feedstocks (dihydroxyacetone, hydroxyacetone) could originate from formose-type chemistry in hydrothermal settings or from serpentinization-driven synthesis of formaldehyde (H2CO), which undergoes aldol condensation to form C3 sugars. This provides a potential hydrothermal \u2192 surface flow of carbon feedstocks.\n\n### Environmental Interconnection\n1. **Hydrothermal \u2192 Surface**: Simple C1 molecules (H2CO, HCN) and C3 sugar derivatives produced at hydrothermal vents or by volcanic outgassing are transported to surface pools where cyanosulfidic chemistry operates.\n2. **Surface \u2192 Biochemical**: L-valine produced in surface cyanosulfidic pools enters the prebiotic amino acid pool available for peptide bond formation and proto-metabolic assembly. Valine is one of the 20 canonical amino acids and a key hydrophobic residue in proteins.\n\n## 7. Open Questions and Challenges\n\n1. **Chirality**: The cyanosulfidic pathway produces racemic valine (equal L and D). The origin of homochirality (biological preference for L-amino acids) remains unresolved for valine. Enantiomeric enrichment mechanisms such as asymmetric adsorption on chiral mineral surfaces, circularly polarized light, or amplification through crystal-liquid equilibria have been proposed but not demonstrated specifically for valine.\n\n2. **Acetone availability**: The pathway requires acetone, which is produced by reductive deoxygenation of sugar derivatives (dihydroxyacetone, hydroxyacetone). The prebiotic availability of these C3 sugars depends on the formose reaction or alternative sugar synthesis routes, whose efficiency and selectivity under prebiotic conditions are debated.\n\n3. **Copper availability**: Elemental copper (Cu\u2070) is essential for the reductive steps. While copper minerals exist in early Earth geological settings, the concentration and bioavailability of dissolved Cu\u2070 in prebiotic surface pools needs quantitative constraints. Copper could be released from sulfide minerals (e.g., chalcopyrite) under the acidic or reducing conditions of cyanosulfidic environments.\n\n4. **Selectivity**: The cyanosulfidic network produces many products simultaneously (amino acids, nucleotide precursors, lipid precursors). The selectivity and yield for valine specifically, versus competing products like leucine (from the C6 aminonitrile pathway) or other branched-chain amino acids, needs further quantification.\n\n5. **Chain extension mechanism**: The reductive rearrangement step (Step 4 of Pathway 1) involving Cu/H2S-mediated chain extension through thioamide intermediates is mechanistically complex and not fully characterized at the molecular level. The detailed radical or polar mechanism of the C\u2013C bond formation during this step remains an active area of study.\n\n6. **Pathway 2 chain shortening**: The conversion of the C6 aminonitrile (2-amino-4-methylpentanenitrile) to C5 valine involves apparent loss of one carbon during hydrolysis. The mechanism of this chain shortening \u2014 whether through retro-aldol, decarboxylation, or another process \u2014 needs clarification.\n\n=== END CURATED LITERATURE REVIEW ===\n\n\n=== END LITERATURE RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\nINSTRUCTIONS FOR LITERATURE-GUIDED SYNTHESIS:\n1. Prefer routes and conditions that are well supported experimentally in the research above.\n2. Weigh the critiques and contested points the research reports; do not adopt a route uncritically.\n3. You may depart from the described routes when the chemistry justifies it; explain why in \"reasoning\".\n4. Cite the supporting paper (author, year) in each reaction's \"reasoning\" field, or mark the reaction as speculative.\n\n\nPREVIOUS FAILED ATTEMPTS - Learn from this feedback:\n============================================================\n\nAttempt 1:\n- Confidence: 0.62\n- Feedback: The network is structurally strong: all 20 molecule nodes and 13 reaction nodes are connected, molecule IDs are consistent, and the ten listed sequences lead formally to the correctly identified racemic valine target. Accepting the deterministic balance check, there are no mass or charge violations. The reported coefficients should be made explicit for rxn_003, rxn_011 and rxn_013; the multiplier required by S8 is not intrinsically suspicious. Chemical viability is nevertheless conditional. rxn_003 lacks a demonstrated mechanism, while the alternative carbon-entry branches depend on unvalidated selective hydrothermal hydrogenation in rxn_005 or dehydrogenation in rxn_006. Established Strecker and hydrolysis chemistry supports the endpoint but does not establish productive operation of the complete network. Surface and Hydrothermal labels do not specify pH, temperature, catalyst phases, hydrogen activity, energy input, residence times or physically justified transfer. Separate compatible settings are acceptable; their number and order are not penalized. Literature alignment is partial: the network correctly rejects the review's C6-to-C5 hydrolysis claim and unsupported L-selectivity, and transparently labels speculation. These are justified corrections, not departures deserving a penalty. However, the internally inconsistent review and its citation to Patel et al. (2015) cannot establish every proposed transformation; reaction-level primary evidence and conditions remain missing. Feedstocks are accepted as declared boundary inputs, not demonstrated supplies. P3 and P5 are effectively tied as the strongest conditional routes, with P3 selected as a representative. Overall confidence reflects shared bottlenecks and integration evidence, not pathway count. The network does not meet the 0.9 approval threshold.\n- Issues: ['The intermediates have the correct carbon counts, and the terminal aminonitrile would yield racemic valine. However, rxn_003 remains a major mechanistic gap: its balanced equation does not establish the required deoxygenation, thioamide conversion and cyanide incorporation. Treating rxn_008 as an equilibrating Strecker reservoir conversion is appropriate. Surface conditions for completing rxn_011 are unspecified.', 'The hydantoin branch does not resolve the unsupported carbon-skeleton transformation in rxn_003. Aminonitrile carbonation and hydantoin formation are consistent with Bucherer\u2013Bergs-type chemistry, but their operation under the proposed surface conditions is not established. Hydantoin hydrolysis can yield valine, although a hydrothermal label alone does not establish complete ring opening and decarbamoylation.', 'The crossed aldol/dehydration constructs the correct methacrolein skeleton, and the downstream cyanohydrin/Strecker sequence is chemically coherent. The principal bottleneck is rxn_005: selective alkene hydrogenation while retaining the aldehyde is plausible with suitable catalysts, but no appropriate mineral phase or operating conditions are supplied. Complete surface nitrile hydrolysis and preservation of reactive aldehydes during transfer also remain conditional.', 'This is a valid conditional carbon-skeleton route with a recognizable hydantoin endpoint. Its main limitations are the unvalidated selective hydrogenation in rxn_005 and insufficient conditions for hydantoin formation and subsequent complete hydrolysis. The lower confidence relative to direct hydrolysis reflects these unresolved chemical requirements, not the additional environment transfer or reaction count.', 'Explicit imine formation and cyanide capture give a mechanistically appropriate Strecker sequence. The imine need not accumulate as an isolated compound, but its transient formation and trapping must compete successfully with hydration and hydrolysis. This pathway is effectively tied with P3 because both share the same upstream hydrogenation bottleneck and downstream aminonitrile hydrolysis requirements.', 'The imine-capture sequence and hydantoin connectivity are chemically appropriate. Nevertheless, rxn_005 lacks an established selective catalyst/environment combination, while rxn_012 and rxn_013 lack conditions demonstrating productive hydantoin formation and complete release of valine. This is an alternative sequence within the same Strecker-based branch, not independent experimental confirmation of the network.', 'Once isobutyraldehyde is available, the downstream sequence is credible Strecker chemistry. The limiting step is rxn_006: aqueous isobutanol does not undergo useful selective dehydrogenation merely because it is heated hydrothermally. A suitable catalyst and favorable hydrogen activity are required; hydrogen-rich conditions would oppose the stated direction. Surface hydrolysis conditions are also missing.', 'The structural sequence reaches the correct hydantoin and could ultimately yield racemic valine. However, selective alcohol dehydrogenation remains unvalidated, and the hydantoin branch requires additional evidence for carbonation and complete hydrolysis under the stated environments. Neither an atom-balanced dehydrogenation equation nor a hydrothermal label establishes productive conversion.', 'The transient imine and cyanide-trapping steps are appropriate for forming the valine aminonitrile. They do not remove the unresolved catalyst and hydrogen-management requirements of rxn_006. This pathway is effectively equivalent in confidence to P7, since the cyanohydrin and imine entries are related equilibrating routes within the same Strecker chemistry.', 'The pathway identifies appropriate aminonitrile and hydantoin intermediates, but target production remains conditional on selective alcohol dehydrogenation and complete hydantoin hydrolysis. The explicit imine entry is mechanistically reasonable but does not constitute independent evidence relative to P8. Compatible conditions and intermediate survival across the proposed settings have not been demonstrated.']\n\n============================================================\nAddress the above issues in your new network. Do NOT repeat the same mistakes.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"Valine\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"Construct racemic valine from the declared boundary feedstocks 2-methylpropanal, HCN, ammonia and water. The aldehyde supplies four carbons and HCN supplies the fifth; aldehyde production is not assumed to have been demonstrated. Two Strecker entries converge on the correct C5 aminonitrile: imine cyanation and cyanohydrin-to-aminonitrile equilibration. Five subsequent sequences represent acidic hydrolysis, alkaline hydrolysis, acidic processing of a sulfide-trapped intermediate, alkaline processing of that intermediate, and a hydantoin branch. Their combinations give ten distinct reaction-node pathways, not ten independently demonstrated prebiotic syntheses; acid/base variants are explicitly identified as condition variants. Direct Strecker hydrolysis is the strongest chemistry. The sulfide and hydantoin branches are lower-confidence environmental hypotheses, included because sulfide and dissolved inorganic carbon can alter aminonitrile fate. The supplied review does not establish ten validated routes. Its C6 aminonitrile would hydrolyze to leucine, not valine, and is excluded. Its insufficiently characterized Cu/H2S carbon-skeleton transformation is also excluded rather than treated as an established reaction. No enantioselectivity is assumed. Conditions below are proposed operating or screening windows, not measurements attributed to Patel et al. (2015). Reactions occur in separate, compatible anoxic surface microenvironments; shaded pores protect intermediates from ambient UV. Neutral parent structures represent species whose actual protonation depends on pH. All listed net reactions balance with unit coefficients; acid-base reservoirs and counterions are not expanded.\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H8O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-methylpropanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Isobutyraldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C4H8O/c1-4(2)3-5/h3-4H,1-2H3\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_002\",\n      \"formula\": \"CHN\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"Hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_003\",\n      \"formula\": \"NH3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"Azane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Ammonia\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"Oxidane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Water\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_005\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2S\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"Sulfane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Hydrogen sulfide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2S/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_006\",\n      \"formula\": \"CO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"Carbon dioxide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Carbon dioxide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CO2/c2-1-3\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_007\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H9N\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-methylpropan-1-imine\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Isobutyraldimine\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H9NO\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-hydroxy-3-methylbutanenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Isobutyraldehyde cyanohydrin\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C5H9NO/c1-4(2)5(7)3-6/h4-5,7H,1-2H3\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_009\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H10N2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-amino-3-methylbutanenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Valine aminonitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C5H10N2/c1-4(2)5(7)3-6/h4-5H,7H2,1-2H3\",\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_010\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H12N2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-amino-3-methylbutanamide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Valinamide\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_011\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H12N2S\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-amino-3-methylbutanethioamide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Valine thioamide\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_012\",\n      \"formula\": \"C6H10N2O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"5-(propan-2-yl)imidazolidine-2,4-dione\",\n      \"common_name\": \"5-Isopropylhydantoin\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_013\",\n      \"formula\": \"C6H12N2O3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-(carbamoylamino)-3-methylbutanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"N-Carbamoylvaline\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_014\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H11NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-amino-3-methylbutanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"DL-Valine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C5H11NO2/c1-3(2)4(6)5(7)8/h3-4H,6H2,1-2H3,(H,7,8)\",\n      \"role\": \"target\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"Reversible formation of the Strecker imine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-315 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Ammonia-rich solution, pH 8.0-9.5; shaded, water-filled pores with restricted gas exchange. Hours-to-days equilibration window. The imine is transient and must be trapped rather than isolated.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ammonia adds to the aldehyde to form a carbinolamine. Reversible dehydration produces an imine/iminium pool, represented by the neutral imine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is established Strecker reaction-class chemistry (Strecker, 1850). Its operation with this boundary aldehyde in a natural pool is a speculative environmental implementation beyond the supplied review, not a newly demonstrated Patel et al. (2015) experiment. Cyanide capture must compete with imine rehydration. No selective alcohol dehydrogenation or alkene hydrogenation is assumed.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanide capture of the Strecker imine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-315 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"pH 8.0-9.5; HCN/CN- and ammonia retained in the same shaded aqueous microenvironment as imine formation. Hours-to-days screening window; no external reductant or irradiation required.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyanide attacks the imine/iminium carbon, followed by proton transfer, giving 2-amino-3-methylbutanenitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Classical Strecker chemistry establishes this bond-forming logic (Strecker, 1850); Patel et al. (2015) supplies relevant prebiotic aminonitrile context rather than measured performance for this proposed setting. The four-carbon imine plus one cyanide carbon gives the correct five-carbon precursor. The achiral setting predicts racemic material. Environmental rates and selectivity remain unverified.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Formation of the isobutyraldehyde cyanohydrin reservoir\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-315 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"pH 8.0-9.2, providing both HCN and nucleophilic CN-. Restricted gas exchange and shading reduce volatile loss and photochemical destruction. Minutes-to-days equilibration window; conversion is not assumed quantitative.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Reversible cyanide addition to the aldehyde carbonyl, followed by protonation of the alkoxide, forms the C5 cyanohydrin.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The product is the C5 hydroxynitrile identified in the supplied review (Patel et al., 2015). Direct formation from its corresponding aldehyde uses ordinary cyanohydrin chemistry and avoids the review's insufficiently established carbon-skeleton rearrangement. This direct environmental entry is a speculative extension beyond the supplied reaction list; aldehyde supply remains a declared boundary condition.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Strecker equilibration of the cyanohydrin with ammonia\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-325 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"pH 8.0-9.5; ammonia-rich aqueous solution with cyanide retained. Hours-to-days screening window. This is an alternative description of entry into an equilibrating Strecker system, not an independent carbon-skeleton synthesis.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The cyanohydrin reversibly releases aldehyde and HCN. Aldehyde-ammonia equilibration gives an imine, which cyanide captures. Released and recaptured HCN cancels from the net equation.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This provides a chemically defensible interpretation of the hydroxynitrile-to-aminonitrile transformation reported in the supplied review (Patel et al., 2015). It does not invoke direct displacement of an unactivated hydroxyl group by ammonia. The imine and cyanohydrin entries share underlying Strecker equilibria, so they are graph-distinct sequences rather than independent experimental confirmations.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Acid-catalyzed aminonitrile hydration to valinamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydronium supplied by an acidic volcanic fluid\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-370 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydronium\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Maintained pH 0-1; water-rich, shaded geothermal pool. Days-to-weeks screening window, not a measured valine-specific rate. Acidification occurs after aminonitrile formation; it is not imposed on the initial Strecker mixture.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Nitrile protonation enables water addition. Proton transfers and imidic-acid-to-amide tautomerization yield valinamide.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The supplied review explicitly identifies an amide intermediate during aminonitrile hydrolysis (Patel et al., 2015), consistent with classical Strecker chemistry (Strecker, 1850). Making that intermediate explicit prevents hydration from being confused with complete amino-acid release. The proposed acidic geothermal window is an environmental extrapolation; productive rates and acid availability require testing.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Acid-catalyzed valinamide hydrolysis\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydronium supplied by an acidic volcanic fluid\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"355-370 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydronium\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Maintained pH 0-1 with excess water; days-to-weeks initial screening window, extended if conversion remains incomplete. Ammonia is actually retained principally as ammonium. Drying is not used during this hydrolysis stage.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Water attacks the protonated amide carbonyl. Collapse of the tetrahedral intermediate cleaves the acyl-nitrogen bond and releases valine and ammonia in neutral-parent notation.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is the chemically necessary second hydrolysis step described in the supplied review's Strecker endpoint (Patel et al., 2015; Strecker, 1850). It can be a terminal kinetic bottleneck. Neither aminonitrile disappearance nor amide accumulation demonstrates completed valine production. Proposed residence times are testing windows, not reported yields or completion times.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Base-promoted aminonitrile hydration to valinamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydroxide in a concentrated alkaline brine\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-370 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydroxide\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"pH 11-12, quoted at 298 K; sustained alkalinity and limited CO2 ingress. Weeks-to-months screening window. Transfer is into a warm alkaline pool rather than the acidic hydrolysis pool.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydroxide attacks the nitrile carbon. Proton transfer and tautomerization of the hydrated nitrile produce the amide.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Base-promoted nitrile hydration is established reaction-class chemistry, but this particular prebiotic operating window is speculative and is not demonstrated by the supplied Patel et al. (2015) review. It represents an alternative hydrolysis regime, not a new carbon-skeleton pathway. Slow hydration, retro-Strecker chemistry and aminonitrile condensation may prevent useful accumulation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Alkaline valinamide hydrolysis\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydroxide alkalinity maintained by an alkaline brine reservoir\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-370 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"pH 11-12, quoted at 298 K; weeks-to-months screening window. Hydroxide is a base promoter and alkalinity is consumed by product speciation. The product is principally valinate under these conditions; neutral valine is the standardized graph species.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydroxide addition to the amide carbonyl and tetrahedral-intermediate breakdown release ammonia. Formation of carboxylate helps drive hydrolysis.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is ordinary amide hydrolysis, but productive valinamide conversion in the proposed brine is a speculative environmental extrapolation beyond Patel et al. (2015). Explicit alkalinity and temperature replace an unsupported generic environment label. The need for sustained base and potentially long residence makes this less secure geochemically than the reaction equation alone suggests.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Sulfide trapping of the valine aminonitrile as a thioamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"pH 8-9 with dissolved H2S/HS- retained; days-to-weeks screening window. Sulfide delivery must exceed removal by abundant dissolved Fe2+. No copper or iron-sulfide catalytic activity is assumed.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrosulfide adds to the nitrile carbon. Proton transfer and tautomerization form 2-amino-3-methylbutanethioamide without changing the carbon skeleton.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative substrate extrapolation: Patel et al. (2015), as summarized in the supplied review, supports sulfide addition to hydroxynitriles, not demonstrated formation of this particular aminothioamide. The branch models a chemically relevant possible fate in sulfide-bearing pools. It is neither a reductive rearrangement nor evidence that sulfur increases valine yield.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Acidic hydrolytic sulfur replacement in valine thioamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydronium supplied by an acidic volcanic fluid\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"340-370 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydronium\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"pH 1-2; days-to-weeks screening window. Transfer into an acidic, water-rich pool. Released H2S can partition into an anoxic headspace; no oxidative sulfur sink is assumed.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Water addition to an activated thiocarbonyl, proton transfer and sulfur elimination replace C=S by C=O, returning the carbon skeleton to valinamide.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative for this substrate. Hydrolytic conversion of thioamides to amides is chemically recognizable, but the supplied Patel et al. (2015) review does not demonstrate this aminothioamide reaction. It is included to test whether sulfide trapping is reversible through hydrolysis. Sulfur replacement, competing C-N cleavage and overall selectivity require experimental determination.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"Alkaline hydrolytic sulfur replacement in valine thioamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydroxide in a concentrated alkaline brine\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-370 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"pH 11-12, quoted at 298 K; days-to-months screening window. Hydroxide is a base promoter. Released sulfur is predominantly HS- rather than molecular H2S; parent-species notation preserves the balanced net transformation.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydroxide attack on the thiocarbonyl followed by proton transfers can replace sulfur with oxygen. Valinamide represents the proposed oxygen-amide intermediate before further alkaline hydrolysis.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative and lower confidence than direct aminonitrile hydrolysis. The supplied Patel et al. (2015) review does not establish this alkaline aminothioamide conversion. Its inclusion evaluates the fate of a sulfide-trapped reservoir under alkaline rather than acidic processing. Competing cleavage pathways could prevent appreciable valinamide accumulation; atom balance does not establish selectivity.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"Bucherer-Bergs-type capture of the aminonitrile as hydantoin\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"315-345 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar with a CO2-containing headspace\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"pH 8-9; ammonium/bicarbonate-rich solution with retained dissolved inorganic carbon. Days-to-weeks screening window. CO2 represents the equilibrating inorganic-carbon reservoir, not its dominant aqueous species.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Aminonitrile carbamylation is followed by intramolecular nitrile capture and heterocycle rearrangement to hydantoin. Both aminonitrile nitrogen atoms are retained. Net incorporation of one CO2 adds the removable carbamoyl carbon.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Laboratory aminonitrile-to-hydantoin chemistry has independent precedent (Bucherer and Steiner, 1934; Ware, 1950). It is a speculative prebiotic extension beyond the supplied Patel et al. (2015) review, not a claimed result of that paper. The balanced net reaction produces no ammonia. Bicarbonate availability does not by itself demonstrate useful hydantoin formation under natural conditions.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_013\",\n      \"name\": \"Alkaline hydantoin ring opening to N-carbamoylvaline\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydroxide alkalinity in a carbonate-poor hyperalkaline microenvironment\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"365-370 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed pH 12.0-12.5, quoted at 298 K; weeks-to-months screening window. This requires an uncommon hyperalkaline water-rock setting and exclusion of substantial CO2 influx. It is separate from the bicarbonate-rich formation pool. These mild-temperature conditions are not demonstrated to give complete conversion.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydroxide attacks a hydantoin carbonyl and opens the ring, producing N-carbamoylvalinate, represented by its neutral parent acid. The carbamoyl protecting group remains attached.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Hydantoin ring opening is established laboratory chemistry (Ware, 1950), but operation in this proposed prebiotic surface window is speculative and absent from the supplied review. Laboratory hydrolysis can require harsher conditions. Explicitly retaining N-carbamoylvaline avoids equating ring opening with free-valine production. Alkali supply, residence time and conversion are unresolved bottlenecks.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_014\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrolytic decarbamoylation to release valine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Hydronium supplied by an acidic volcanic fluid\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"365-370 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Hydronium\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Maintained pH 0-1 after neutralization of the incoming alkaline solution; weeks-to-months screening window. Episodic volcanic-acid influx or transport into an adjacent acidic pool supplies the required pH change. CO2 loss can assist product removal; ammonia remains principally ammonium.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrolysis removes the N-carbamoyl group. Its inorganic products are ammonia and carbon dioxide, leaving the original C5 amino-acid skeleton intact.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Free amino-acid release from hydantoin-derived carbamoyl compounds is recognized laboratory chemistry (Ware, 1950), but productive decarbamoylation in this surface window is speculative and unsupported by the supplied Patel et al. (2015) review. Hydantoin re-formation competes under acidic conditions. This explicit terminal bottleneck means the hydantoin branch cannot be presented as a demonstrated complete prebiotic route.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Imine-entry Strecker synthesis with acidic hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Declared isobutyraldehyde forms a transient imine, cyanide supplies the fifth carbon, and sequential acidic nitrile hydration and amide hydrolysis release racemic valine. One of the strongest reaction-class routes; natural feedstock delivery and productive residence times remain unestablished.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_010\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanohydrin-entry Strecker synthesis with acidic hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"The C5 cyanohydrin acts as a reversible aldehyde/cyanide reservoir before ammonia-driven Strecker equilibration and acidic hydrolysis. Comparable in chemical support to P1, but not an independent Strecker mechanism.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_010\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Imine-entry Strecker synthesis with alkaline hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Imine cyanation is followed by nitrile and amide hydrolysis in an alkaline brine. This is a condition-resolved alternative to P1, not a different carbon-skeleton synthesis. Sustained alkalinity and useful hydrolysis rates are conditional.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_010\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanohydrin-entry Strecker synthesis with alkaline hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Cyanohydrin equilibration supplies the aminonitrile, which is processed through valinamide under alkaline conditions. This combines the reservoir entry of P2 with the conditional hydrolysis regime of P3.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_010\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"Imine entry through a sulfide reservoir and acidic hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"The Strecker aminonitrile is provisionally trapped as an aminothioamide. Acidic sulfur replacement returns it to valinamide, followed by valine release. The aminothioamide formation and recovery steps are speculative substrate-specific extensions.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_006\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_010\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanohydrin entry through a sulfide reservoir and acidic hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"A cyanohydrin reservoir feeds Strecker amination, followed by proposed sulfide trapping and acidic recovery through valinamide. It shares the unvalidated aminothioamide bottlenecks of P5.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_006\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_010\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"Imine entry through a sulfide reservoir and alkaline hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Imine-derived aminonitrile undergoes proposed thioamide trapping, alkaline sulfur replacement and valinamide hydrolysis. This is an alkaline condition variant of the sulfur-reservoir route; selective sulfur replacement is particularly uncertain.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_008\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_010\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanohydrin entry through a sulfide reservoir and alkaline hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"Cyanohydrin-mediated Strecker entry is followed by the same speculative alkaline aminothioamide processing as P7. The sequence is graph-distinct but supplies no independent evidence for those shared reactions.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_008\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_010\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"Imine-entry hydantoin route with explicit deprotection\",\n      \"description\": \"Imine-derived aminonitrile incorporates a temporary CO2-derived carbamoyl carbon to form hydantoin. Separate ring opening and decarbamoylation restore the C5 valine skeleton. This is a conditional laboratory-grounded branch, not a demonstrated prebiotic sequence; both deprotection stages require validation.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_012\", \"rxn_013\", \"rxn_014\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanohydrin-entry hydantoin route with explicit deprotection\",\n      \"description\": \"Cyanohydrin-mediated Strecker equilibration precedes hydantoin capture, alkaline ring opening and acidic decarbamoylation. The lost carbon is specifically the added carbamoyl carbon, unlike the review's unsupported C6 aminonitrile shortening. The same unresolved hydrolysis and environmental-integration limitations as P9 apply.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_012\", \"rxn_013\", \"rxn_014\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_013\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_011\"],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_009\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_004\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_005\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_010\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_005\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_006\", \"rxn_008\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_014\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_006\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_014\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": []\n    }\n  ]\n}", "parsed_response": {"target_molecule": "Valine", "overall_strategy": "Construct racemic valine from the declared boundary feedstocks 2-methylpropanal, HCN, ammonia and water. The aldehyde supplies four carbons and HCN supplies the fifth; aldehyde production is not assumed to have been demonstrated. Two Strecker entries converge on the correct C5 aminonitrile: imine cyanation and cyanohydrin-to-aminonitrile equilibration. Five subsequent sequences represent acidic hydrolysis, alkaline hydrolysis, acidic processing of a sulfide-trapped intermediate, alkaline processing of that intermediate, and a hydantoin branch. Their combinations give ten distinct reaction-node pathways, not ten independently demonstrated prebiotic syntheses; acid/base variants are explicitly identified as condition variants. Direct Strecker hydrolysis is the strongest chemistry. The sulfide and hydantoin branches are lower-confidence environmental hypotheses, included because sulfide and dissolved inorganic carbon can alter aminonitrile fate. The supplied review does not establish ten validated routes. Its C6 aminonitrile would hydrolyze to leucine, not valine, and is excluded. Its insufficiently characterized Cu/H2S carbon-skeleton transformation is also excluded rather than treated as an established reaction. No enantioselectivity is assumed. Conditions below are proposed operating or screening windows, not measurements attributed to Patel et al. (2015). Reactions occur in separate, compatible anoxic surface microenvironments; shaded pores protect intermediates from ambient UV. Neutral parent structures represent species whose actual protonation depends on pH. All listed net reactions balance with unit coefficients; acid-base reservoirs and counterions are not expanded.", "molecules": [{"id": "mol_001", "formula": "C4H8O", "iupac_name": "2-methylpropanal", "common_name": "Isobutyraldehyde", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C4H8O/c1-4(2)3-5/h3-4H,1-2H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_002", "formula": "CHN", "iupac_name": "Hydrogen cyanide", "common_name": "Hydrogen cyanide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_003", "formula": "NH3", "iupac_name": "Azane", "common_name": "Ammonia", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_004", "formula": "H2O", "iupac_name": "Oxidane", "common_name": "Water", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_005", "formula": "H2S", "iupac_name": "Sulfane", "common_name": "Hydrogen sulfide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2S/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_006", "formula": "CO2", "iupac_name": "Carbon dioxide", "common_name": "Carbon dioxide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CO2/c2-1-3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_007", "formula": "C4H9N", "iupac_name": "2-methylpropan-1-imine", "common_name": "Isobutyraldimine", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_008", "formula": "C5H9NO", "iupac_name": "2-hydroxy-3-methylbutanenitrile", "common_name": "Isobutyraldehyde cyanohydrin", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C5H9NO/c1-4(2)5(7)3-6/h4-5,7H,1-2H3", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_009", "formula": "C5H10N2", "iupac_name": "2-amino-3-methylbutanenitrile", "common_name": "Valine aminonitrile", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C5H10N2/c1-4(2)5(7)3-6/h4-5H,7H2,1-2H3", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_010", "formula": "C5H12N2O", "iupac_name": "2-amino-3-methylbutanamide", "common_name": "Valinamide", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_011", "formula": "C5H12N2S", "iupac_name": "2-amino-3-methylbutanethioamide", "common_name": "Valine thioamide", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_012", "formula": "C6H10N2O2", "iupac_name": "5-(propan-2-yl)imidazolidine-2,4-dione", "common_name": "5-Isopropylhydantoin", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_013", "formula": "C6H12N2O3", "iupac_name": "2-(carbamoylamino)-3-methylbutanoic acid", "common_name": "N-Carbamoylvaline", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_014", "formula": "C5H11NO2", "iupac_name": "2-amino-3-methylbutanoic acid", "common_name": "DL-Valine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C5H11NO2/c1-3(2)4(6)5(7)8/h3-4H,6H2,1-2H3,(H,7,8)", "role": "target", "environment_formed": "Surface"}], "reactions": [{"id": "rxn_001", "name": "Reversible formation of the Strecker imine", "inputs": ["mol_001", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_007", "mol_004"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-315 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Ammonia-rich solution, pH 8.0-9.5; shaded, water-filled pores with restricted gas exchange. Hours-to-days equilibration window. The imine is transient and must be trapped rather than isolated."}, "mechanism": "Ammonia adds to the aldehyde to form a carbinolamine. Reversible dehydration produces an imine/iminium pool, represented by the neutral imine.", "reasoning": "This is established Strecker reaction-class chemistry (Strecker, 1850). Its operation with this boundary aldehyde in a natural pool is a speculative environmental implementation beyond the supplied review, not a newly demonstrated Patel et al. (2015) experiment. Cyanide capture must compete with imine rehydration. No selective alcohol dehydrogenation or alkene hydrogenation is assumed."}, {"id": "rxn_002", "name": "Cyanide capture of the Strecker imine", "inputs": ["mol_007", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_009"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-315 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "pH 8.0-9.5; HCN/CN- and ammonia retained in the same shaded aqueous microenvironment as imine formation. Hours-to-days screening window; no external reductant or irradiation required."}, "mechanism": "Cyanide attacks the imine/iminium carbon, followed by proton transfer, giving 2-amino-3-methylbutanenitrile.", "reasoning": "Classical Strecker chemistry establishes this bond-forming logic (Strecker, 1850); Patel et al. (2015) supplies relevant prebiotic aminonitrile context rather than measured performance for this proposed setting. The four-carbon imine plus one cyanide carbon gives the correct five-carbon precursor. The achiral setting predicts racemic material. Environmental rates and selectivity remain unverified."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Formation of the isobutyraldehyde cyanohydrin reservoir", "inputs": ["mol_001", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_008"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-315 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "pH 8.0-9.2, providing both HCN and nucleophilic CN-. Restricted gas exchange and shading reduce volatile loss and photochemical destruction. Minutes-to-days equilibration window; conversion is not assumed quantitative."}, "mechanism": "Reversible cyanide addition to the aldehyde carbonyl, followed by protonation of the alkoxide, forms the C5 cyanohydrin.", "reasoning": "The product is the C5 hydroxynitrile identified in the supplied review (Patel et al., 2015). Direct formation from its corresponding aldehyde uses ordinary cyanohydrin chemistry and avoids the review's insufficiently established carbon-skeleton rearrangement. This direct environmental entry is a speculative extension beyond the supplied reaction list; aldehyde supply remains a declared boundary condition."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Strecker equilibration of the cyanohydrin with ammonia", "inputs": ["mol_008", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_009", "mol_004"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-325 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "pH 8.0-9.5; ammonia-rich aqueous solution with cyanide retained. Hours-to-days screening window. This is an alternative description of entry into an equilibrating Strecker system, not an independent carbon-skeleton synthesis."}, "mechanism": "The cyanohydrin reversibly releases aldehyde and HCN. Aldehyde-ammonia equilibration gives an imine, which cyanide captures. Released and recaptured HCN cancels from the net equation.", "reasoning": "This provides a chemically defensible interpretation of the hydroxynitrile-to-aminonitrile transformation reported in the supplied review (Patel et al., 2015). It does not invoke direct displacement of an unactivated hydroxyl group by ammonia. The imine and cyanohydrin entries share underlying Strecker equilibria, so they are graph-distinct sequences rather than independent experimental confirmations."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Acid-catalyzed aminonitrile hydration to valinamide", "inputs": ["mol_009", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_010"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydronium supplied by an acidic volcanic fluid"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-370 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "Hydronium", "aqueous": true, "other": "Maintained pH 0-1; water-rich, shaded geothermal pool. Days-to-weeks screening window, not a measured valine-specific rate. Acidification occurs after aminonitrile formation; it is not imposed on the initial Strecker mixture."}, "mechanism": "Nitrile protonation enables water addition. Proton transfers and imidic-acid-to-amide tautomerization yield valinamide.", "reasoning": "The supplied review explicitly identifies an amide intermediate during aminonitrile hydrolysis (Patel et al., 2015), consistent with classical Strecker chemistry (Strecker, 1850). Making that intermediate explicit prevents hydration from being confused with complete amino-acid release. The proposed acidic geothermal window is an environmental extrapolation; productive rates and acid availability require testing."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Acid-catalyzed valinamide hydrolysis", "inputs": ["mol_010", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_014", "mol_003"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydronium supplied by an acidic volcanic fluid"], "conditions": {"temperature": "355-370 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "Hydronium", "aqueous": true, "other": "Maintained pH 0-1 with excess water; days-to-weeks initial screening window, extended if conversion remains incomplete. Ammonia is actually retained principally as ammonium. Drying is not used during this hydrolysis stage."}, "mechanism": "Water attacks the protonated amide carbonyl. Collapse of the tetrahedral intermediate cleaves the acyl-nitrogen bond and releases valine and ammonia in neutral-parent notation.", "reasoning": "This is the chemically necessary second hydrolysis step described in the supplied review's Strecker endpoint (Patel et al., 2015; Strecker, 1850). It can be a terminal kinetic bottleneck. Neither aminonitrile disappearance nor amide accumulation demonstrates completed valine production. Proposed residence times are testing windows, not reported yields or completion times."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Base-promoted aminonitrile hydration to valinamide", "inputs": ["mol_009", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_010"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydroxide in a concentrated alkaline brine"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-370 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "Hydroxide", "aqueous": true, "other": "pH 11-12, quoted at 298 K; sustained alkalinity and limited CO2 ingress. Weeks-to-months screening window. Transfer is into a warm alkaline pool rather than the acidic hydrolysis pool."}, "mechanism": "Hydroxide attacks the nitrile carbon. Proton transfer and tautomerization of the hydrated nitrile produce the amide.", "reasoning": "Base-promoted nitrile hydration is established reaction-class chemistry, but this particular prebiotic operating window is speculative and is not demonstrated by the supplied Patel et al. (2015) review. It represents an alternative hydrolysis regime, not a new carbon-skeleton pathway. Slow hydration, retro-Strecker chemistry and aminonitrile condensation may prevent useful accumulation."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Alkaline valinamide hydrolysis", "inputs": ["mol_010", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_014", "mol_003"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydroxide alkalinity maintained by an alkaline brine reservoir"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-370 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "pH 11-12, quoted at 298 K; weeks-to-months screening window. Hydroxide is a base promoter and alkalinity is consumed by product speciation. The product is principally valinate under these conditions; neutral valine is the standardized graph species."}, "mechanism": "Hydroxide addition to the amide carbonyl and tetrahedral-intermediate breakdown release ammonia. Formation of carboxylate helps drive hydrolysis.", "reasoning": "This is ordinary amide hydrolysis, but productive valinamide conversion in the proposed brine is a speculative environmental extrapolation beyond Patel et al. (2015). Explicit alkalinity and temperature replace an unsupported generic environment label. The need for sustained base and potentially long residence makes this less secure geochemically than the reaction equation alone suggests."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "Sulfide trapping of the valine aminonitrile as a thioamide", "inputs": ["mol_009", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_011"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "pH 8-9 with dissolved H2S/HS- retained; days-to-weeks screening window. Sulfide delivery must exceed removal by abundant dissolved Fe2+. No copper or iron-sulfide catalytic activity is assumed."}, "mechanism": "Hydrosulfide adds to the nitrile carbon. Proton transfer and tautomerization form 2-amino-3-methylbutanethioamide without changing the carbon skeleton.", "reasoning": "Speculative substrate extrapolation: Patel et al. (2015), as summarized in the supplied review, supports sulfide addition to hydroxynitriles, not demonstrated formation of this particular aminothioamide. The branch models a chemically relevant possible fate in sulfide-bearing pools. It is neither a reductive rearrangement nor evidence that sulfur increases valine yield."}, {"id": "rxn_010", "name": "Acidic hydrolytic sulfur replacement in valine thioamide", "inputs": ["mol_011", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_005"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydronium supplied by an acidic volcanic fluid"], "conditions": {"temperature": "340-370 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "Hydronium", "aqueous": true, "other": "pH 1-2; days-to-weeks screening window. Transfer into an acidic, water-rich pool. Released H2S can partition into an anoxic headspace; no oxidative sulfur sink is assumed."}, "mechanism": "Water addition to an activated thiocarbonyl, proton transfer and sulfur elimination replace C=S by C=O, returning the carbon skeleton to valinamide.", "reasoning": "Speculative for this substrate. Hydrolytic conversion of thioamides to amides is chemically recognizable, but the supplied Patel et al. (2015) review does not demonstrate this aminothioamide reaction. It is included to test whether sulfide trapping is reversible through hydrolysis. Sulfur replacement, competing C-N cleavage and overall selectivity require experimental determination."}, {"id": "rxn_011", "name": "Alkaline hydrolytic sulfur replacement in valine thioamide", "inputs": ["mol_011", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_005"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydroxide in a concentrated alkaline brine"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-370 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "pH 11-12, quoted at 298 K; days-to-months screening window. Hydroxide is a base promoter. Released sulfur is predominantly HS- rather than molecular H2S; parent-species notation preserves the balanced net transformation."}, "mechanism": "Hydroxide attack on the thiocarbonyl followed by proton transfers can replace sulfur with oxygen. Valinamide represents the proposed oxygen-amide intermediate before further alkaline hydrolysis.", "reasoning": "Speculative and lower confidence than direct aminonitrile hydrolysis. The supplied Patel et al. (2015) review does not establish this alkaline aminothioamide conversion. Its inclusion evaluates the fate of a sulfide-trapped reservoir under alkaline rather than acidic processing. Competing cleavage pathways could prevent appreciable valinamide accumulation; atom balance does not establish selectivity."}, {"id": "rxn_012", "name": "Bucherer-Bergs-type capture of the aminonitrile as hydantoin", "inputs": ["mol_009", "mol_006"], "outputs": ["mol_012"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "315-345 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar with a CO2-containing headspace", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "pH 8-9; ammonium/bicarbonate-rich solution with retained dissolved inorganic carbon. Days-to-weeks screening window. CO2 represents the equilibrating inorganic-carbon reservoir, not its dominant aqueous species."}, "mechanism": "Aminonitrile carbamylation is followed by intramolecular nitrile capture and heterocycle rearrangement to hydantoin. Both aminonitrile nitrogen atoms are retained. Net incorporation of one CO2 adds the removable carbamoyl carbon.", "reasoning": "Laboratory aminonitrile-to-hydantoin chemistry has independent precedent (Bucherer and Steiner, 1934; Ware, 1950). It is a speculative prebiotic extension beyond the supplied Patel et al. (2015) review, not a claimed result of that paper. The balanced net reaction produces no ammonia. Bicarbonate availability does not by itself demonstrate useful hydantoin formation under natural conditions."}, {"id": "rxn_013", "name": "Alkaline hydantoin ring opening to N-carbamoylvaline", "inputs": ["mol_012", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_013"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydroxide alkalinity in a carbonate-poor hyperalkaline microenvironment"], "conditions": {"temperature": "365-370 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Proposed pH 12.0-12.5, quoted at 298 K; weeks-to-months screening window. This requires an uncommon hyperalkaline water-rock setting and exclusion of substantial CO2 influx. It is separate from the bicarbonate-rich formation pool. These mild-temperature conditions are not demonstrated to give complete conversion."}, "mechanism": "Hydroxide attacks a hydantoin carbonyl and opens the ring, producing N-carbamoylvalinate, represented by its neutral parent acid. The carbamoyl protecting group remains attached.", "reasoning": "Hydantoin ring opening is established laboratory chemistry (Ware, 1950), but operation in this proposed prebiotic surface window is speculative and absent from the supplied review. Laboratory hydrolysis can require harsher conditions. Explicitly retaining N-carbamoylvaline avoids equating ring opening with free-valine production. Alkali supply, residence time and conversion are unresolved bottlenecks."}, {"id": "rxn_014", "name": "Hydrolytic decarbamoylation to release valine", "inputs": ["mol_013", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_014", "mol_003", "mol_006"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Hydronium supplied by an acidic volcanic fluid"], "conditions": {"temperature": "365-370 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 bar", "catalyst": "Hydronium", "aqueous": true, "other": "Maintained pH 0-1 after neutralization of the incoming alkaline solution; weeks-to-months screening window. Episodic volcanic-acid influx or transport into an adjacent acidic pool supplies the required pH change. CO2 loss can assist product removal; ammonia remains principally ammonium."}, "mechanism": "Hydrolysis removes the N-carbamoyl group. Its inorganic products are ammonia and carbon dioxide, leaving the original C5 amino-acid skeleton intact.", "reasoning": "Free amino-acid release from hydantoin-derived carbamoyl compounds is recognized laboratory chemistry (Ware, 1950), but productive decarbamoylation in this surface window is speculative and unsupported by the supplied Patel et al. (2015) review. Hydantoin re-formation competes under acidic conditions. This explicit terminal bottleneck means the hydantoin branch cannot be presented as a demonstrated complete prebiotic route."}], "pathways": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "name": "Imine-entry Strecker synthesis with acidic hydrolysis", "description": "Declared isobutyraldehyde forms a transient imine, cyanide supplies the fifth carbon, and sequential acidic nitrile hydration and amide hydrolysis release racemic valine. One of the strongest reaction-class routes; natural feedstock delivery and productive residence times remain unestablished.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_005", "rxn_006"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_007", "mol_009", "mol_010"]}, {"pathway_id": "P2", "name": "Cyanohydrin-entry Strecker synthesis with acidic hydrolysis", "description": "The C5 cyanohydrin acts as a reversible aldehyde/cyanide reservoir before ammonia-driven Strecker equilibration and acidic hydrolysis. Comparable in chemical support to P1, but not an independent Strecker mechanism.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_003", "rxn_004", "rxn_005", "rxn_006"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_008", "mol_009", "mol_010"]}, {"pathway_id": "P3", "name": "Imine-entry Strecker synthesis with alkaline hydrolysis", "description": "Imine cyanation is followed